var tafsirData_12 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lãm-Ra\" opens this chapter with the same three disconnected letters that began the previous one, recited individually rather than as a word. Several chapters share this feature, and the honest position taken by many early scholars is that their complete meaning rests with Allah. A frequently made observation is that these are ordinary letters of the Arabic alphabet, the same building blocks the Arabs used in their celebrated poetry, placed at the head of a Book they were challenged to imitate and could not match. The materials were entirely familiar; the result was not.\n\n\"These are the verses of the clear Book\" introduces what follows. The Arabic word \"mubin\" carries two related meanings that both apply. It describes something that is itself clear, plain, and unambiguous, and it describes something that makes other things clear. Both fit the Quran, which is clear in itself and clarifies matters that human beings could never settle on their own: the purpose of creation, the nature of God, and what happens after death.\n\nThe description is especially fitting at the start of this particular chapter. What follows is the story of Joseph, told in a single continuous narrative from beginning to end, which is unusual in the Quran. Most prophetic accounts are distributed across different chapters and told in pieces, each time emphasising what suits the context. Here the account runs unbroken through more than a hundred verses.\n\nThat clarity of storytelling is part of the point. Nothing in the chapter is obscure. A listener with no religious training can follow the events of a boy's dream, his brothers' jealousy, the well, the caravan, the house of the Egyptian minister, the prison, and the eventual reunion. The lessons emerge from the events themselves rather than from abstract argument.\n\nComing immediately after a chapter filled with warnings and destroyed nations, this opening signals a change in method. The previous chapter argued; this one narrates, and the reader is about to be taught through a story rather than through a series of confrontations.",
  "2": "\"Indeed, We have sent it down as an Arabic Quran so that you may understand\" explains why the revelation came in the language it did, and the reason given is entirely practical: comprehension.\n\nThe Arabic wording states the purpose plainly. A message intended to guide a people must reach them in words they actually use. Revelation in an unfamiliar language would have been an obstacle rather than a mercy, and Surah Fussilat makes exactly this point, noting that had it been revealed as a non-Arabic Quran, they would have objected that its verses were not made clear in their own language.\n\nThis connects with a broader principle the Quran states elsewhere, that no messenger was sent except in the language of his own people, so that he could make things clear to them. The first audience for any revelation must be able to understand it without an interpreter standing between them and their Lord.\n\nMentioning the Arabic language here also relates to the challenge running through the previous chapters. The Quran was delivered in the language its opponents had mastered, in a society that prized eloquence above almost everything, and they were invited to produce something comparable. Choosing their own field of expertise made the challenge sharper.\n\nThere is a question this verse raises for the vast majority of Muslims today, who do not speak Arabic. If the purpose of the Arabic Quran is understanding, what does that mean for a Nigerian, Indonesian, or English-speaking believer? The answer is that the verse establishes understanding as the goal, which makes the effort to understand a religious duty rather than an optional extra. Translation, study, and learning something of the original language are all ways of pursuing what this verse names as the purpose. Reciting without any attempt at comprehension falls short of what the verse itself describes as the reason for revelation.\n\nPlacing this statement just before the story of Joseph is fitting. The chapter is about to deliver a long narrative whose value depends entirely on the listener following what happens and why.",
  "3": "\"We relate to you ˹O Prophet˺ the best of stories through Our revelation of this Quran\" introduces the narrative that fills this chapter, and the description given to it is unusual. The Arabic phrase indicates the most beautiful or finest of accounts, and commentators have explained the description in several complementary ways.\n\nIt may be the best because of how completely it is told, running from a boy's dream to its fulfilment decades later without interruption. It may be the best because of the range of human experience it contains: jealousy between brothers, a father's grief, temptation, false accusation, imprisonment, the interpretation of dreams, the rise to authority, and finally forgiveness. It may also be described this way because of the lessons packed into it, since almost every moral situation a person can face appears somewhere in its course.\n\nWhat should be clear is what the description does not mean. It is not a story in the sense of something invented. The chapter is presenting real events, and the word used for relating them indicates recounting something that occurred.\n\n\"Though before this you were totally unaware ˹of them˺\" makes a claim that was open to immediate contradiction. The Prophet had never studied scripture, could not read, and had spent his life in Mecca among people equally unfamiliar with these accounts in detail. The version of Joseph's story preserved among the Jewish communities of the region differs in significant ways from what this chapter presents, which makes the suggestion of borrowing difficult to sustain.\n\nThere is also a reported context for this chapter's revelation, mentioned by commentators, that some of the Prophet's companions asked him to relate something to them, or that questioners tested him about the story of the Children of Israel who went to Egypt. Such reports vary in strength, and the chapter itself does not depend on them.\n\nWhat the verse establishes is that this narrative arrived as revelation, delivered to a man who had no access to it by ordinary means.",
  "4": "\"˹Remember˺ when Joseph said to his father, \"O my dear father! Indeed I dreamt of eleven stars, and the sun, and the moon—I saw them prostrating to me!\"\" begins the narrative with a child speaking to his father in the most affectionate terms. The Arabic form of address he uses is tender, the way a young son speaks to a father he trusts completely, and that closeness matters for everything that follows.\n\nJoseph was the son of Jacob, who was the son of Isaac, who was the son of Abraham. This is a family with three generations of prophethood behind it, and the chapter will later state that the favour given to Abraham and Isaac was being completed in this line.\n\nThe dream itself is precise. Eleven stars, the sun, and the moon, all bowing toward him. The number is not incidental. Joseph had eleven brothers, and the interpretation becomes clear at the end of the chapter, when his parents and brothers finally stand before him in Egypt and he says that this is the fulfilment of the vision he saw long before.\n\nProphetic dreams are treated seriously in Islam. The Prophet Muhammad said, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim, that a good dream is one of the forty-six parts of prophethood, and his own revelation began with true dreams that came like the breaking of dawn. Joseph's dream was of that kind, a genuine indication of the future rather than the ordinary confusion of sleep.\n\nWhat makes the opening so effective as storytelling is that the reader is given the ending at the beginning. Everything that happens next, the well, the slavery, the prison, appears to move away from this dream, and every step is in fact moving toward it. The listener watches events that look like disasters, knowing already how the story concludes.\n\nJacob's response in the next verse shows that he understood immediately what his son had been shown, and understood equally well what would happen if the wrong people heard about it.",
  "5": "\"He replied, \"O my dear son! Do not relate your vision to your brothers\"\" is Jacob's immediate response to what his son has just described, and the tenderness of his address matches the way Joseph had spoken to him. The Arabic form he uses is affectionate, a father speaking gently while giving serious advice.\n\nNotice what Jacob does not say. He does not dismiss the dream as childish imagination, and he does not tell Joseph he has misunderstood it. He acts at once on the assumption that the dream is true and important, which tells us he recognised it as more than ordinary sleep. As a prophet himself, he knew what such a vision meant, and he also knew his own household.\n\n\"Or they will devise a plot against you\" gives the reason for the secrecy. Jacob understood that a dream announcing his youngest son's future eminence would not be received as good news by ten older brothers who already resented the attention Joseph received. The Arabic word for their plot indicates a scheme worked out with care, and this is exactly what the following verses describe them doing.\n\nThere is practical wisdom here that reaches far beyond this family. Not everything true needs to be announced. A person may receive a blessing, an opportunity, or a piece of good fortune, and telling everyone is not always wise. The Prophet Muhammad advised in a hadith recorded by at-Tabarani that people should seek help in fulfilling their needs through discretion, and it is well known that envy is stirred by display. Jacob was not teaching his son to be deceitful; he was teaching him not to place temptation in front of people who were already struggling with jealousy.\n\n\"Surely Satan is a sworn enemy to humankind\" names the deeper danger. Jacob does not describe his other sons as evil. He identifies the enemy who works on human weakness, turning ordinary jealousy into something far worse. The Arabic word \"mubin\" here indicates an enmity that is open and undisguised, declared from the beginning and never hidden.",
  "6": "\"And so will your Lord choose you ˹O Joseph˺\" continues Jacob's response, moving from warning to interpretation. Having told his son to keep the dream private, he now explains what it signifies. The Arabic verb indicates selecting someone, picking him out for a particular purpose, and it points to prophethood.\n\nJacob reads the dream correctly from the first moment. Stars, sun, and moon bowing before a boy could only mean that this child would be raised to a position of honour, and a prophet's son in a prophetic household would understand what kind of honour was meant.\n\n\"And teach you the interpretation of dreams\" identifies the specific gift Joseph would be given. The Arabic phrase refers to the interpretation of statements and events, which commentators explained as including the meaning of dreams and the understanding of how matters unfold. This becomes the thread running through the entire chapter. Joseph interprets the dreams of two prisoners, then the dream of the king, and that ability is the door through which he rises from a prison cell to authority over Egypt.\n\n\"And perfect His favour upon you and the descendants of Jacob\" extends the blessing beyond Joseph himself to the whole family. This mattered, because the family was about to be torn apart by what the brothers were planning.\n\n\"˹Just˺ as He once perfected it upon your forefathers, Abraham and Isaac\" places Joseph in a line. Abraham was his great-grandfather, Isaac his grandfather, Jacob his father. Four generations of prophethood in one family is remarkable, and the mention here serves to remind Joseph that what is coming to him is part of something already established.\n\n\"Surely your Lord is All-Knowing, All-Wise\" closes with two names that fit the situation exactly. He knows who is fit for what He gives, and He places His gifts with perfect wisdom. Both names would be tested in Joseph's life, since almost everything that happened to him for the next several years looked like the opposite of favour.",
  "7": "\"Indeed, in the story of Joseph and his brothers there are lessons for all who ask\" pauses the narrative before it properly begins and tells the listener why it is being told. The Arabic word for these lessons indicates signs, indications, evidence pointing to something beyond itself. What follows is not simply a family drama.\n\nThe phrase \"for all who ask\" identifies the audience who will benefit. Commentators have understood this in more than one way. It may mean those who ask questions, who investigate and want to understand. It may refer specifically to the questioners who prompted this chapter, since reports mention that some people asked the Prophet about the story of Joseph, or about how the Children of Israel came to be in Egypt. Both readings point to the same principle: the person who comes looking for meaning finds it, while the person who listens idly hears only a sequence of events.\n\nMentioning \"Joseph and his brothers\" together in the summary is deliberate. This is not only Joseph's story. The brothers are central, and their journey from jealousy to plotting, from deception to eventual shame and forgiveness, carries as much instruction as Joseph's own rise.\n\nThe signs in this chapter are of several kinds. There are signs of Allah's planning, since every apparent disaster in the story turns out to be a step toward the fulfilment of the opening dream. There are signs about human nature, since jealousy, temptation, false accusation, and grief all appear in recognisable forms. And there is a sign in the very existence of the narrative, delivered by a man who had no access to it, which the chapter mentioned in the preceding verses.\n\nFor the Prophet's first listeners in Mecca, the relevance was immediate. Here was an account of a man rejected and harmed by his own family, sold, imprisoned on a false charge, and eventually raised to a position from which he could forgive them all. A community facing hostility from its own tribe was being shown how such stories end.",
  "8": "\"˹Remember˺ when they said ˹to one another˺, \"Surely Joseph and his brother ˹Benjamin˺ are more beloved to our father than we\"\" opens the brothers' conversation, and it begins with a complaint that is entirely honest. They felt less loved, and they were not imagining it.\n\nJacob did favour Joseph and his full brother Benjamin. The two were the youngest, born to the same mother, who had died. Commentators note that a father's particular tenderness toward young children who have lost their mother is natural, and Jacob also knew, from the dream and from his own insight, that Joseph carried something special. None of this made the older brothers' feelings unreal.\n\n\"Even though we are a group of so many\" reveals what they thought entitled them to more. They were numerous, older, and useful. In a household that depended on flocks and labour, ten grown sons were the family's strength. By their calculation, love should have been distributed according to usefulness.\n\n\"Indeed, our father is clearly mistaken\" is the sentence where a feeling becomes an accusation. They pass judgement on a prophet, declaring that his affection is misplaced. The Arabic word here indicates being in clear error, and commentators are careful to note that they meant error of judgement rather than religious misguidance, since they still recognised him as their father and prophet.\n\nThis verse repays careful attention because it traces how wrongdoing begins. It does not start with a plan to harm anyone. It starts with a comparison, then a grievance, then a justification. Once they had decided their father was in the wrong, whatever they did next could be presented to themselves as correcting a wrong rather than committing one.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad warned in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim against envy, telling believers to avoid it because it consumes good deeds as fire consumes wood. He also instructed parents, in a well-known incident recorded in the same collections, to treat their children equally in giving, precisely because favouritism plants exactly this kind of resentment.",
  "9": "\"\"Kill Joseph or cast him out to some ˹distant˺ land\" is where resentment turns into a proposal, and the speed of that turn is frightening. In the previous verse they were complaining about their father's affection. Here they are discussing murder.\n\nTwo options are offered, and both aim at the same result: removing Joseph permanently from their father's sight. The second option, sending him far away, is only marginally less brutal than the first, since a boy abandoned in a distant land in that world had little chance of survival or return.\n\n\"So that our father's attention will be only ours\" states the motive plainly. The Arabic indicates that their father's face, meaning his attention, affection, and regard, would then be free for them alone. This is worth pausing over, because it shows how confused their thinking had become. They imagined that destroying the person their father loved would transfer that love to them. Anyone can see that killing a beloved child would fill a father with grief, not affection, and the story goes on to show exactly that. Jacob's heart remained with Joseph for decades, and he wept until his sight failed.\n\nEnvy makes poor calculations. It focuses so completely on removing what another person has that it never checks whether removal will actually deliver what the envious person wants.\n\n\"Then after that you may ˹repent and˺ become righteous people!\" is the most chilling part of the plan. They schedule their repentance in advance. Commit the crime first, and become righteous afterwards.\n\nThis deserves clear comment, because the reasoning still appears in different forms. A person decides to take unlawful earnings now and give charity later, or to postpone prayer until an easier stage of life. Planning to sin while relying on future repentance corrupts the repentance itself, since genuine turning back involves regret, and a person who has already arranged his regret has not truly regretted anything. Nobody is guaranteed the time in which to carry out such a plan.",
  "10": "\"\"Do not kill Joseph\" comes from one of the brothers, and this single voice changes the outcome of the entire story. The Quran does not name him here, though commentators have suggested it was the eldest, and other verses later describe an eldest brother who refused to leave Egypt without permission from their father. Whoever he was, he was part of the plot and yet unwilling to go as far as the others.\n\n\"But if you must do something, throw him into the bottom of a well\" is his alternative, and it should not be mistaken for goodness. He is still proposing that a child be dropped into a pit and abandoned. What he offers is a lesser evil within a plan he has not rejected.\n\n\"So perhaps he may be picked up by some travellers\" reveals his actual hope. The well he had in mind was on a route where caravans stopped for water. A boy found there would most likely be taken and sold, which is exactly what happened. In his mind, Joseph would be gone from their father's life, which satisfied the group's aim, without blood on their hands.\n\nThe Quran preserves this detail carefully, and it is worth reflecting on why. This partial restraint saved Joseph's life, and everything that followed, the rise in Egypt, the survival of the family during famine, the reunion, all depended on it. A man with mixed motives and insufficient courage still made a difference by refusing the worst option.\n\nThat observation cuts two ways. It shows that even a small resistance to wrongdoing matters, and it shows that partial resistance is not innocence. This brother participated in taking a child from his father, lying about his fate, and watching an old man grieve for years. He was not blameless.\n\nBehind all of it, the pattern that runs through the whole chapter is already visible. The brothers were arranging Joseph's disappearance, and the arrangement they settled on was precisely the route that carried him to Egypt.",
  "11": "\"\"O our father! Why do you not trust us with Joseph, although we truly wish him well?\" begins the second stage of the plan, and it shows how carefully the brothers thought about their approach. They do not demand. They complain gently, as sons who feel unfairly suspected.\n\nThe question is cleverly framed. By asking why he does not trust them, they force Jacob into an awkward position. Any father accused of distrusting his own sons feels pressure to prove otherwise, and a direct refusal would sound like an admission that he considers them dangerous.\n\nThe claim that they wish Joseph well is the most striking part. The Arabic word indicates sincere goodwill, wanting genuine benefit for someone. They had discussed killing him days or moments earlier. This is deception operating at its smoothest, using the exact language of affection to arrange a crime.\n\nThe Quran preserves the wording precisely because this pattern is so recognisable. Harm rarely announces itself. It arrives wrapped in concern, in reasonable requests, in the appearance of hurt feelings. Someone who wishes to manipulate a decent person almost always appeals to that person's sense of fairness.\n\nThere is an implied question here about Jacob himself. He was a prophet, and he had already warned Joseph not to share his dream with these very brothers. Did he not see through them? The chapter suggests he was uneasy, and his reply in the next verse expresses exactly that unease. But prophets are not given knowledge of the unseen except what Allah discloses to them, and Jacob was in the position of any father who suspects something without proof. Refusing outright would have meant accusing his sons of intending harm, based on nothing he could point to.\n\nThe chapter presents this whole exchange without commentary, letting the words speak. Readers already know what the brothers said to each other, so every phrase of their appeal carries a double meaning that Jacob could not hear.",
  "12": "\"\"Send him out with us tomorrow so that he may enjoy himself and play\" is the specific request, and the brothers frame it around Joseph's own benefit. They present themselves as thinking of the boy, offering him a day outdoors after what they imply has been too much confinement at home.\n\nThe appeal is well judged. A father who loves his son wants that son to be happy, and refusing a simple day of play sounds harsh. There was nothing suspicious in the request on its face; young boys in a herding family would naturally go out with their older brothers to the pastures.\n\n\"And we will really watch over him\" adds the guarantee, and the Arabic carries emphasis, as though anticipating exactly the objection Jacob is about to raise. They promise protection in advance of being asked for it.\n\nThat detail is worth noticing. People who are telling the truth usually answer the concerns that are put to them. People who have prepared a deception often answer concerns before they are raised, because they have rehearsed the conversation. The brothers had thought this through.\n\nThe mention of play also serves their plan practically. A day of games among the flocks explains why the group might scatter, why Joseph might be left alone for a while, and why an accident could occur without anyone being blamed. When they return with their story in a few verses, the detail of having gone racing fits neatly with the outing they requested here.\n\nThere is a broader observation in this exchange about how families can be destroyed from within. Everything here is ordinary: brothers asking to take a younger sibling out, a father hesitating, an assurance being given. The evil is entirely hidden inside a completely normal conversation.\n\nFor the listener, the tension of the chapter comes from knowing what Jacob does not. His reluctance, expressed in the next verse, turns out to be closer to the truth than anything his sons say, though even he does not guess what is actually being planned.",
  "13": "\"He responded, \"It would truly sadden me if you took him away with you\"\" is Jacob's answer, and it begins with feeling rather than argument. He does not accuse them of anything. He simply says that being separated from Joseph would grieve him.\n\nThere is something quietly revealing in this. Jacob had no evidence against his sons, so he could not object on grounds of suspicion. What he could say honestly was that he did not want to be parted from the boy. A father is allowed to feel that.\n\n\"And I fear that a wolf may devour him while you are negligent of him\" gives the second reason, and this sentence becomes important later. Wolves were a genuine hazard in grazing country, so the concern was reasonable in itself. What matters is that Jacob was the first to mention a wolf.\n\nCommentators have noticed this, and some have observed that his own words handed the brothers their alibi. When they return in a few verses, the story they tell is precisely the scenario he described. Whether they had already planned that excuse or simply took the one he offered, his fear supplied the shape of their lie.\n\nThere is a lesson here that is worth stating carefully, without turning it into superstition. Speaking one's fears aloud in front of people who may exploit them is not always wise. Jacob's warning was honest and reasonable, and it was used against him.\n\nNotice also the second half of his fear: \"while you are negligent of him.\" He does not imagine they would harm Joseph deliberately. His worry is that they would become absorbed in their own activity and stop paying attention. Even at his most anxious, he thinks the worst of them is carelessness.\n\nThat detail deepens the tragedy of the chapter. Jacob's suspicion, such as it was, fell far short of the truth. He was worried about a lapse of attention while his sons were discussing whether to kill his child.",
  "14": "\"\"If a wolf were to devour him, despite our strong group, then we would certainly be losers!\" is the brothers' reply, and it is a masterpiece of misdirection. They take their father's stated fear and treat it as an insult to their competence.\n\nThe argument they make is about numbers and strength. They are a substantial group of grown men, well able to handle a predator. For a wolf to take a boy from among them would mean they were useless, and they express this as an unthinkable disgrace. The Arabic word they use for what they would be, losers, indicates people who have failed completely and lost everything of worth.\n\nThe reasoning is sound as far as it goes, and this is what makes it effective. Jacob cannot dispute that ten men could fend off a wolf. What he cannot say aloud, and perhaps could not fully articulate to himself, is that the danger he sensed was not coming from wildlife.\n\nThere is a bitter irony running through the sentence. They declare that they would be losers if a wolf ate Joseph, and they were about to become losers in a far more serious sense by throwing him into a well and lying about it. The word they chose to describe an unthinkable failure became an accurate description of what they made themselves.\n\nTheir appeal to group strength is worth noticing too, because it echoes what they said in their private conversation. There they complained that their father favoured Joseph even though they were a group of so many. Here they present that same numerical strength as a guarantee of safety. The thing they resented not being valued for is the thing they now offer as security.\n\nJacob is given no reply in the text. The next verse moves straight to the outing, which suggests he agreed, unable to justify a refusal. A father with a bad feeling and no evidence let his son go, which is a situation many people recognise.",
  "15": "\"And so, when they took him away and decided to throw him into the bottom of the well\" moves the narrative forward without describing the journey, the games, or the moment the pretence dropped. The Quran leaves those details out, and the omission is effective. What matters is that agreement became action.\n\nThe wording indicates that they agreed together on this. Whatever hesitation the one brother had shown earlier, the group carried out the plan. Other passages of scripture and later commentary describe the boy pleading, the shirt being taken from him, and his terror as he was lowered down. None of that is in the Quran, and it is better not to add to what has been revealed.\n\n\"We inspired him: \"˹One day˺ you will remind them of this deed of theirs while they are unaware ˹of who you are˺.\"\" is the extraordinary turn in this verse. At the very bottom of the well, in the moment of complete helplessness, Joseph receives a communication from his Lord.\n\nThe message is not a promise of immediate rescue. It is something much stranger and much stronger: information about the future. A day is coming when he will stand in front of these same brothers and remind them of what they did here, and they will not even recognise him. That scene occurs later in this chapter, when the brothers come to Egypt seeking food and speak to a powerful official without knowing who he is.\n\nConsider what this meant for the boy in the well. He now knew, with certainty, that he would survive, that he would see his brothers again, and that the position would be reversed. Everything between that moment and its fulfilment, the slavery, the false accusation, the years in prison, would be endured by someone who had been told how it ends.\n\nThis is one of the central themes of the chapter. Allah's help often arrives not as an escape from difficulty but as a certainty carried through it.",
  "16": "\"Then they returned to their father in the evening, weeping\" shifts the scene from the well to the family tent, and the detail chosen is the tears.\n\nThe timing is mentioned deliberately. They came back in the evening, at the hour when a father would be waiting for his sons and the boy he had reluctantly let go. Evening is when a household gathers, and Joseph's absence would be immediately visible.\n\nThe weeping is the part that has struck commentators most. These were men who had spent the day carrying out a plan they had discussed in cold detail, including the possibility of murder. On the way home they produced tears convincing enough to present to a grieving father.\n\nThere is a well-known observation attributed to early scholars that tears are not proof of sincerity. A person can weep from guilt, from fear of being caught, or simply from the effort of performing. The Quran records the tears without endorsing them, and the next verses show Jacob refusing to be persuaded by the display.\n\nWhat makes this verse quietly devastating is the ordinariness of it. A family evening, sons returning, sounds of crying. Everything about the scene would look, to any observer, like a genuine tragedy being reported. The only person in the story who knows otherwise is the boy at the bottom of a well, and the reader.\n\nThere is also something worth noticing about how the Quran narrates this. It does not pause to condemn them or to describe their hearts. It simply reports what they did, and lets the contrast between their earlier conversation and their present tears do the work. This restraint is characteristic of the chapter, which trusts the listener to draw conclusions rather than supplying them at every step.\n\nTheir words follow immediately, and those words contain a slip that a careful listener, and their father, would catch.",
  "17": "\"\"Our father! We went racing and left Joseph with our belongings, and a wolf devoured him!\" is the story they had prepared, and it repeats almost exactly the scenario Jacob himself had described. He had feared a wolf while they were negligent, and they present precisely that: a race, an unattended boy, a predator.\n\nThe detail about racing serves their account well. It explains the negligence without making them appear reckless, since young men racing on foot or on horseback in open country is entirely ordinary. It also creates distance between them and the moment of the supposed attack.\n\n\"But you will not believe us, no matter how truthful we are\" is the sentence that gives them away. Nobody who has just delivered an honest report anticipates disbelief in this manner. Truthful people are usually shocked when they are doubted; they do not build the doubt into their statement in advance.\n\nTheir words also carry an unintended admission. They speak of themselves being truthful in a moment when they are lying about the most serious thing they have ever done, and the phrase sits awkwardly in the mouth of anyone who knows what happened. Commentators have long pointed to this line as evidence of a guilty conscience speaking before it is questioned.\n\nThere is a broader observation here about deception. Elaborate stories tend to contain one detail too many. A person telling the truth reports what happened and stops; a person constructing an account adds reinforcements, anticipates objections, and manages the listener's reaction. Each addition is a small crack.\n\nThe claim itself was also risky. A wolf attack leaves evidence: a body, remains, torn ground. They produce none of this, only a garment, which the next verse examines.\n\nTheir father's reply shows that he was not persuaded. He does not argue with the wolf story or interrogate them about the details. He responds to something much deeper than the surface of their account, and his answer is one of the most remarkable statements in the chapter.",
  "18": "\"And they brought his shirt, stained with false blood\" is the piece of evidence they thought would settle the matter, and it is the detail that undid them. The Arabic describes blood that was false, meaning it was real blood but not Joseph's, taken from an animal to support their story.\n\nCommentators have noted the obvious problem with this exhibit, and several early scholars mention it explicitly: the shirt was bloodied but not torn. A wolf that devours a boy does not remove his shirt neatly first. Whether Jacob reasoned in exactly that way or simply knew his sons, he was not convinced.\n\n\"He responded, \"No! Your souls must have tempted you to do something ˹evil˺.\"\" is his verdict. The Arabic indicates that their inner selves made something appear attractive and acceptable to them, so that they went along with it. He does not accuse them of a specific crime, because he does not know what they did. He identifies the mechanism: they had allowed themselves to be talked into something by their own desires.\n\n\"So ˹I can only endure with˺ beautiful patience!\" is the phrase this verse is most remembered for. The Arabic expression indicates patience without complaint to people, without bitterness, without collapse. Commentators explained beautiful patience as patience in which there is no complaining to anyone except Allah, which is exactly what Jacob demonstrates later when he says that he complains of his grief only to Allah.\n\nIt is important to see what this does and does not mean. Jacob wept. His grief was so intense that he eventually lost his sight from it. Beautiful patience is not the absence of pain or the suppression of tears; it is refusing to let pain turn into resentment against Allah or into bitter accusation against people.\n\n\"It is Allah's help that I seek to bear your claims\" completes his response. He has no way to prove they are lying, no power to recover his son, and no ally in his own household. He turns to the only source of help available.",
  "19": "\"And there came some travellers, and they sent their water-boy who let down his bucket into the well\" resumes the story at the place the brothers left it. A caravan stops at a known watering point, an ordinary task is performed, and everything changes.\n\nThe detail is completely mundane. A man lowering a bucket for water is the least remarkable event imaginable in desert travel. That is precisely the point. The rescue arrives through a routine action by a person who had no idea he was part of anything significant.\n\n\"He cried out, \"Oh, what a great find! Here is a boy!\"\" captures his reaction. The Arabic exclamation expresses delighted surprise, and his delight was not about saving a life. A boy pulled from a well in that world was an unclaimed asset, and his first thought was of value.\n\n\"And they took him secretly ˹to be sold˺ as merchandise\" states what they intended. They concealed him, treating him as goods to be moved and sold rather than as a person to be returned to a family. Commentators have differed slightly over who hid him from whom, with some suggesting the water-boy and his companions hid the discovery from the rest of the caravan so as not to share the profit. Either way, Joseph passed from a well into the hands of traders.\n\nConsider the sequence being built here. Joseph was thrown into a pit by his brothers to be rid of him. A caravan found him and, out of greed, carried him to Egypt. Every person acting in this chain was pursuing his own selfish interest, and every one of them was moving Joseph precisely toward the position that the dream had promised.\n\n\"But Allah is All-Knowing of what they did\" closes the verse with the perspective that the participants lacked. They thought they had made a lucky discovery and a private arrangement. Nothing about it was hidden, and nothing about it was outside a plan far larger than their profit.",
  "20": "\"They ˹later˺ sold him for a cheap price, just a few silver coins\" completes the transaction, and the Quran emphasises how little he fetched. The Arabic indicates a reduced, deficient price, and the coins are described as few and counted out, which suggests a small, quickly settled sum.\n\nThere is a deliberate contrast running through this verse. The person being sold was a prophet's son, the great-grandson of Abraham, a boy who would one day govern the wealth of Egypt. The price was pocket change.\n\nCommentators have long drawn out the lesson in that gap. Value in the marketplace has nothing to do with worth before Allah. People are bought, dismissed, underestimated, and priced by others constantly, and none of that assessment carries weight where it matters. Joseph was sold cheaply because his sellers had no idea what they were holding.\n\n\"Only wanting to get rid of him\" explains why the price was so low. The Arabic indicates that they had no interest in him and wished to be done with the matter. Several reasons suggest themselves and commentators have mentioned them: a boy of unknown origin was risky property, since a family might appear and claim him, and holding stolen goods invites trouble. They wanted a quick sale rather than a good one.\n\nThere is an echo here of what the brothers wanted. They too had simply wanted Joseph gone, and were willing to accept any outcome that achieved it. Two separate groups, with different motives, both treating him as a problem to be removed.\n\nMeanwhile the trajectory continues. A cheap sale in an Egyptian market put Joseph into the household of a senior official rather than into hard labour in a field. The very indifference of his sellers placed him where he needed to be. The next verse names the buyer and reveals, for the first time in the story, what all of this was actually accomplishing.",
  "21": "\"The man from Egypt who bought him said to his wife, \"Take good care of him, perhaps he may be useful to us or we may adopt him as a son.\"\" introduces the household that would shape the next stage of Joseph's life. The buyer is not named in the Quran, though he is identified in the chapter by his position as a man of standing, and later verses refer to him as the one whose wife attempted the seduction.\n\nHis instruction is striking. He does not tell his wife to put the boy to work. He tells her to honour his stay and treat him well, and he speculates that they might one day take him as a son. Commentators have noted that the couple appear to have been childless, which explains the thought.\n\nSomething about Joseph clearly impressed him immediately. A slave bought cheaply at market does not normally prompt talk of adoption. Whatever the man saw, his instinct was to treat this boy differently.\n\n\"This is how We established Joseph in the land\" is the verse's turning point, and it reframes everything that has happened. The pit, the caravan, the cheap sale, the Egyptian house: the Quran gathers all of it into one act of establishment. What looked like a series of misfortunes was a route.\n\n\"So that We might teach him the interpretation of dreams\" gives the purpose. Joseph needed to be in Egypt, in a household connected to the ruling class, to learn what he would later need. His education for leadership was beginning inside someone else's home, as a purchased boy.\n\n\"Allah's Will always prevails, but most people do not know\" states the principle behind the whole chapter. The Arabic indicates that Allah is dominant, overpowering in His affair, so that His purpose is carried out regardless of who opposes it. Ten brothers plotted, traders schemed, and every one of them served a plan they knew nothing about. The closing phrase explains why this comfort is so rarely felt: most people simply do not know how the world is being run.",
  "22": "\"And when he reached maturity, We gave him wisdom and knowledge\" passes over Joseph's youth in a single line. Years in the Egyptian household are compressed into the word maturity, and what the Quran chooses to report about that period is the gift he received at the end of it.\n\nThe Arabic term for maturity indicates full physical and mental strength, the point at which a person's development is complete. Commentators discussed the age intended, with suggestions ranging around the thirties, and there is no need to fix it precisely.\n\nTwo things were given, and they belong together. The Arabic word \"hukm,\" rendered here as wisdom, indicates sound judgement and the ability to decide correctly between competing considerations. Knowledge is the material; wisdom is the capacity to use it well. A person may know a great deal and still choose badly, and a person with good instincts but no knowledge will act sincerely in the wrong direction.\n\nNotice what is not mentioned. Nothing is said about wealth, status, or comfort. Joseph was still a servant in another man's house, with no rights over his own life. What he was given was internal, and it was the only equipment he would need for what came next.\n\n\"This is how We reward the good-doers\" attaches the gift to a principle. The Arabic word \"muhsinin\" describes those who do things well and beautifully, with excellence in both conduct and worship. The Prophet Muhammad explained this quality in a hadith recorded by Muslim as worshipping Allah as though you see Him, and knowing that if you do not see Him, He sees you.\n\nApplied to Joseph, this tells us something the narrative has not stated directly. During those quiet years as a servant, he had been doing well. Nobody was watching him except the household, and yet he conducted himself in a way that earned this description.\n\nThe very next verse tests exactly that quality, in the hardest way possible.",
  "23": "\"And the lady, in whose house he lived, tried to seduce him\" introduces the trial that defines this stage of Joseph's life. The Quran describes her by her position rather than her name, and the phrase emphasises the situation: he was living in her house, under her authority, with nowhere else to go.\n\nEverything about the circumstances was against him. He was a servant with no protector, no family in the country, and no standing to accuse anyone. Refusing a powerful woman in her own home carried obvious danger, and the rest of the chapter shows exactly what that danger was.\n\n\"She locked the doors ˹firmly˺ and said, \"Come to me!\"\" describes the moment with two details. The doors were secured, which removed both the possibility of interruption and the possibility of escape, and her invitation was direct.\n\n\"He replied, \"Allah is my refuge!\"\" is his first response, and it is worth noticing what it is not. He does not begin by discussing consequences, reputation, or risk. He seeks protection with Allah, which is the instinct of someone whose relationship with his Lord is already established. Temptation is not usually defeated in the moment it arrives; it is defeated by what a person has built beforehand.\n\n\"It is ˹not right to betray˺ my master, who has taken good care of me\" is his second reason. The man of the house had treated him honourably, as the earlier verse described, and Joseph refuses to repay that with betrayal. Gratitude and decency are being invoked alongside faith, which shows that a believer's moral reasoning includes his obligations to people.\n\n\"Indeed, the wrongdoers never succeed\" states the principle that settles it. The Arabic word for success here refers to genuine flourishing, and Joseph's judgement is that no act of wrongdoing ever leads there, whatever it appears to offer in the moment.\n\nThe Prophet mentioned among those who will be shaded on the Day of Judgment a man who is invited by a woman of position and beauty and says that he fears Allah, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim.",
  "24": "\"She advanced towards him, and he would have done likewise, had he not seen a sign from his Lord\" is the most carefully discussed sentence in the chapter, and it deserves an honest explanation, because careless readings have caused unnecessary confusion.\n\nThe Arabic uses the same verb for both of them, which is why some translations render it as each having an inclination. Commentators have offered several explanations. Many held that her intent was a settled resolve to act, while whatever stirred in Joseph was the natural pull that any healthy young man would feel, which he did not act upon and which was checked immediately. Others, including well-known early authorities, explained the structure of the Arabic sentence itself as meaning that he would have inclined had he not seen the sign, so that the sign prevented the inclination from ever forming into intent.\n\nWhat is agreed among reliable scholars is that Joseph committed no sin. The chapter states this repeatedly. He fled, he called on Allah, he was declared innocent by witnesses, by the women of the city, and eventually by the woman herself. Later in this chapter he says openly that Allah does not guide the plot of the treacherous, and the king declares him trustworthy.\n\nWhat the sign was is not specified. Reports mention various suggestions, and none of them is established with certainty. The Quran leaves it undescribed, and the honest position is to accept that Allah showed him something that settled the matter.\n\n\"This is how We kept evil and indecency away from him\" gives the outcome and identifies who did the keeping. Joseph resisted, and the Quran attributes the protection to Allah. Both are true at once, and this is how the Quran usually presents human effort and divine help.\n\n\"For he was truly one of Our chosen servants\" explains why that protection came. He was selected, purified, devoted to Allah. Sincerity of that kind is itself a defence, which is why Satan is elsewhere quoted admitting that he has no power over Allah's sincere servants.",
  "25": "\"They raced for the door and she tore his shirt from the back\" describes the moment Joseph's refusal turned into flight. He did not stay to argue or negotiate. He moved for the exit, and she pursued him, catching his garment as he ran.\n\nThe direction of the tear is the crucial detail, and the Quran mentions it here before anyone uses it as evidence. A shirt torn at the back belongs to a man running away from someone behind him. This physical fact becomes the turning point of the whole episode.\n\nJoseph's decision to flee is worth pausing over. He had already refused verbally, seeking refuge with Allah and invoking his duty to the man who had honoured him. When refusal was not accepted, he removed himself physically. This is the pattern Islamic teaching consistently recommends for temptation: distance rather than debate. A person who stays in the room to test his own strength has already lost part of the battle.\n\n\"Only to find her husband at the door\" is the sudden reversal. The door she had locked opens onto the one person neither of them expected. In an instant a private confrontation becomes a public accusation.\n\n\"She cried, \"What is the penalty for someone who tried to violate your wife, except imprisonment or a painful punishment?\"\" shows how quickly she moved. There is no pause, no confusion, no attempt to explain. She reverses the accusation completely and, in the same breath, proposes the sentence.\n\nHer speed is instructive. She frames Joseph as the aggressor and herself as the victim, and she does so before he can say a word. Notice also that she suggests the punishments rather than demanding a hearing. Someone genuinely wronged usually asks for justice; she was managing an outcome.\n\nThe vulnerability of Joseph's position is total. A foreign servant with no family, no standing, and no witnesses, accused by the mistress of the house in front of her husband. Everything about the situation was arranged against him, and the only thing on his side was the truth and a torn piece of cloth.",
  "26": "\"\"It was she who tried to seduce me\" is Joseph's entire defence, and its brevity is remarkable. He is facing an accusation that could end his life, and he answers in a single sentence.\n\nNotice what he does not do. He does not become emotional, does not insult her, does not describe details of the encounter, and does not plead. He states the truth plainly and stops. A person who is innocent has no story to construct, so he has nothing to elaborate.\n\nThe Arabic wording places the emphasis on her, correcting the direction of the accusation exactly.\n\n\"And a witness from her own family testified\" introduces an unexpected voice. Commentators have differed about who this was, and the difference is worth mentioning honestly. Many understood the witness to be a relative of hers, a man of judgement present in the household, who offered a way to settle the matter. Some early reports suggested other possibilities, including an unusual report about an infant speaking, but the plain reading of the verse is that a member of her own family gave testimony.\n\nWhat makes this significant is the source. The person suggesting a test came from her side, not from Joseph's. He had no family in Egypt and no advocate. When the deciding argument arrived, it came from the household of the woman accusing him.\n\n\"If his shirt is torn from the front, then she has told the truth and he is a liar\" sets out the first half of the test, and its logic is simple and sound. A man attacking a woman who resists would be facing her, and any tearing would be at the front as she pushed him away.\n\nThis method of reasoning deserves notice. The witness does not ask who seems more trustworthy or who has higher status. He proposes examining physical evidence and letting it decide. In a situation where a powerful woman's word stood against a servant's, someone insisted on looking at the facts.",
  "27": "\"\"But if it is torn from the back, then she has lied and he is truthful\" completes the test proposed in the previous verse. Each possibility has been assigned its meaning in advance, before anyone has looked at the garment.\n\nThat sequencing matters enormously, and it is the mark of an honest procedure. The rule was announced first and applied afterwards. Nobody could adjust the interpretation to fit a preferred conclusion, because both outcomes had already been stated in front of everyone present.\n\nThe reasoning behind this half is the mirror of the first. A man fleeing from someone who is pursuing him would be caught from behind, and the fabric would give way at the back. The physical evidence cannot be argued with in the way that words can.\n\nThere is a broader principle here that Islamic teaching later developed at length. Accusations require evidence, and circumstantial indications carry real weight in establishing what happened. Muslim jurists discussed cases of exactly this kind, where physical signs point to the truth in the absence of witnesses, and several cited this very passage in doing so.\n\nIt is also worth noticing how the test protects both parties. Had the shirt been torn at the front, Joseph would have been convicted by the same method that eventually cleared him. He was not being offered a way to escape; he was being offered a fair examination, which is all an innocent person needs.\n\nFor readers today the passage carries an application that hardly needs spelling out. Accusations of serious misconduct are among the hardest things any community deals with. The Quran here models something valuable: do not decide by status, by sympathy, or by who spoke first. Look at what can actually be established, agree in advance what it would mean, and then look.\n\nThe result of the examination follows immediately, and with it the collapse of her account.",
  "28": "\"So when her husband saw that Joseph's shirt was torn from the back\" reports the examination and, with it, the verdict. The evidence pointed exactly where the witness had said it would. Joseph was innocent, and the accusation had been an invention.\n\n\"He said ˹to her˺, \"This must be ˹an example˺ of the cunning of you ˹women˺! Indeed, your cunning is so shrewd!\"\" is the husband's reaction, and it needs careful handling, because it has often been misused.\n\nThese are the words of the Egyptian minister, not a statement from Allah about women in general. The Quran is reporting what a man said in a moment of anger and embarrassment on discovering his wife's conduct. Reading it as a divine verdict on half of humanity contradicts the Quran itself, which repeatedly addresses believing men and believing women together and states that they are supporters of one another, and which praises women such as Mary and the wife of Pharaoh in the strongest terms. It is worth being explicit about this, because the verse has sometimes been quoted as though it were a general ruling, which it plainly is not.\n\nWhat the remark does capture is the particular quality of the scheme he had just uncovered: a plan carried out through appearance, timing, and immediate accusation, of the kind that works precisely because it is difficult to disprove. The Arabic word \"kayd\" indicates a subtle plan, a manoeuvre designed to achieve something indirectly. The chapter uses this word of the brothers' plotting as well, and elsewhere of the women of the city.\n\nThere is something revealing in his focus. Having discovered a serious betrayal in his own house, he responds with a comment about cleverness rather than with any real reckoning. His handling of the matter, in the next verse, continues in the same direction: contain it, minimise it, and move on.",
  "29": "\"\"O Joseph! Forget about this.\"\" is the first half of the minister's response once the truth is established, and it is a request for silence. He asks the wronged party to turn away from the matter and not speak of it.\n\nConsider what is happening. A man has just been falsely accused of a serious crime, cleared by evidence, and his response is to be told to drop it. No apology is offered. No compensation is mentioned. The interest of the household, and the reputation of a powerful family, has been placed above the interest of the person who was wronged.\n\n\"And you ˹O wife˺! Seek forgiveness for your sin.\" is the second half, addressed to the guilty party. He does name it as a sin, which is something, and he tells her to ask forgiveness. Whether he meant forgiveness from Allah or simply from him is not stated, though the wording suggests genuine wrongdoing acknowledged.\n\n\"It certainly has been your fault.\" settles responsibility clearly. He does not pretend the matter was ambiguous.\n\nYet the punishment he had heard proposed a few moments earlier, imprisonment or painful punishment, is not mentioned again. The person who suggested those penalties for an innocent man receives an instruction to seek forgiveness, while the innocent man receives an instruction to keep quiet.\n\nThis is a picture of how power protects itself, and the Quran records it without comment. What follows in the chapter shows the consequence of that inadequate response. The matter was not settled, the household continued as before, gossip spread through the city, and Joseph eventually ended up in the prison that had been threatened here.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the way the chapter handles injustice. Joseph is cleared and still suffers. Being proven right does not always produce fairness, and the story does not pretend otherwise. What it shows instead is a man who keeps his conduct upright while other people mishandle their responsibilities around him.",
  "30": "\"Some women of the city gossiped, \"The Chief Minister's wife is trying to seduce her slave-boy.\"\" moves the story out of the household and into the streets of the capital. What the minister tried to contain has escaped, exactly as such things do.\n\nThe identity of the speakers matters. These were women of the city's elite, the social circle surrounding the minister's household. Their interest is not moral concern. It is the pleasure of discussing a scandal involving someone above them.\n\n\"Love for him has plagued her heart\" is a vivid expression. The Arabic image is of love reaching the innermost membrane of the heart, penetrating to its very lining. They are saying she is completely overtaken.\n\n\"Indeed, we see that she is clearly mistaken\" is their verdict, and the phrasing repays attention. The same expression, describing someone as being in clear error, was used earlier in this chapter by Joseph's brothers about their father. In both cases people pass confident judgement on someone else's situation from the outside.\n\nTheir criticism is also revealing in what it targets. They do not condemn her for attempting adultery as a violation of what is right. Their complaint is that she has debased herself with a servant, someone beneath her station. Their moral scale is entirely social, and this becomes obvious in the next verses, when they meet Joseph and their own composure collapses.\n\nThe Quran includes this episode for a reason. Gossip of this kind spreads faster than any correction, and the person least able to defend himself in it was Joseph. His name was now attached to a scandal throughout the city, without any opportunity to explain, and the eventual result was a prison sentence for a crime he had refused to commit.\n\nIslamic teaching treats backbiting with severity for exactly these reasons. The Prophet described it, in a hadith recorded by Muslim, as mentioning about your brother what he would dislike, and confirmed that it remains backbiting even when what is said is true.",
  "31": "\"When she heard about their gossip, she invited them and set a banquet for them\" shows the minister's wife responding to criticism with a plan of her own. She does not deny the rumour or defend herself in words. She arranges a demonstration.\n\nThe invitation is a social occasion, elegant and entirely ordinary on the surface. The women who had been discussing her privately are now her guests, seated comfortably in her home.\n\n\"She gave each one a knife\" is the detail that reveals the plan. Knives were provided for the food, which is unremarkable in itself, and it means every guest had a blade in her hand at the moment of the reveal. She had thought this through.\n\n\"Then said ˹to Joseph˺, \"Come out before them.\"\" is the moment. Joseph, who had no choice in the matter, is displayed to a room full of women who had been mocking his mistress for wanting him.\n\n\"When they saw him, they were so stunned ˹by his beauty˺ that they cut their hands\" is the result. They were so overwhelmed that they injured themselves without noticing. The Prophet mentioned Joseph's remarkable appearance in the report of the Night Journey, saying he had been given a share of beauty, in a narration recorded by Muslim.\n\n\"And exclaimed, \"Good God! This cannot be human; this must be a noble angel!\"\" completes their reaction. They abandon their earlier judgement completely.\n\nWhat this episode exposes is worth stating clearly. These women had condemned her confidently, certain they would never behave so foolishly. Presented with the same test for a few seconds, they lost control of their own hands. The scene is a lesson about the ease of judging others and the difficulty of holding one's own composure under the same pressure.\n\nNone of this excuses her. The point is not that temptation removes responsibility, and Joseph himself is the proof standing in front of them. He faced this constantly and did not yield.",
  "32": "\"\"This is the one for whose love you criticized me!\" is her triumph in front of the women whose hands are bleeding. Her plan worked exactly as intended. She has not been cleared of anything, but she has proved that her critics were in no position to condemn her.\n\n\"I did try to seduce him but he ˹firmly˺ refused.\" is a startling admission, and its significance is easy to miss. She confesses openly, in front of a room of the city's leading women, that she was the one who pursued him and that he refused. This is a public declaration of Joseph's innocence from the only person who could give it, and it is preserved in the Quran.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that he held himself back, protecting himself, remaining chaste. She is describing his integrity while boasting of her attempt.\n\n\"And if he does not do what I order him to, he will certainly be imprisoned and ˹fully˺ disgraced.\" reveals what her confession was building toward. Having demonstrated that any woman would have done the same, she now announces that she intends to continue, and she states the threat openly.\n\nThis is the moment when the story turns dark again. She is no longer attempting seduction; she is applying force through her power over him. Joseph's options are reduced to submitting to what is forbidden or accepting a prison sentence.\n\nNotice that she says all of this in front of witnesses, and none of them objects. Commentators have observed that the women, far from being appalled, appear to have added their own pressure on Joseph afterwards, which is why his prayer in the next verse speaks of the cunning of a group rather than of one person.\n\nJoseph is now facing a situation with no worldly escape. Every human being with power in the room is on the wrong side, and the only route out that would satisfy them all is the one thing he will not do.",
  "33": "\"Joseph prayed, \"My Lord! I would rather be in jail than do what they invite me to.\"\" is his response to the threat, and it is one of the clearest statements of priority in the Quran.\n\nHe is choosing prison. Not accepting it reluctantly as a fate imposed on him, but stating that he prefers it. Between a comfortable life purchased through disobedience and a cell that preserves his integrity, he considers the cell the better outcome.\n\nNotice the plural in \"what they invite me to.\" It is no longer one woman. The women of the city, who had criticised her and then been silenced by his appearance, appear to have joined the pressure. He is facing a coordinated push from an entire social circle.\n\n\"And if You do not turn their cunning away from me, I might yield to them\" is the part of the prayer that shows his character most clearly. A man who has just refused repeatedly, fled from a locked room, and declared his willingness to go to prison now admits that he could fall.\n\nThis is not false modesty. It is accurate self-knowledge. Joseph understood that his resistance so far was not a guarantee of resistance tomorrow, and that constant pressure wears people down. Anyone who has struggled with a persistent temptation recognises the truth of this.\n\nThe lesson is important and easily missed. The strongest people in matters of temptation are the ones who do not trust their own strength. Confidence in one's own resolve is itself a danger, which is why the Prophet frequently prayed to Allah to keep his heart firm upon the religion, in a supplication recorded in the hadith collections.\n\n\"And fall into ignorance\" names what yielding would make him. The Arabic term indicates behaving without knowledge or restraint, acting like someone who does not know better. Sin, in this framing, is not sophistication or freedom. It is a form of foolishness, whatever the surrounding culture calls it.",
  "34": "\"So his Lord responded to him, turning their cunning away from him\" is the answer to the prayer just offered, and it comes immediately in the text. Joseph had asked for exactly two things: protection from their scheming and preservation from falling. Both were granted.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates that Allah responded to his call, which is the language the Quran uses for accepted supplication. There is no delay described here, no long period of waiting. This is worth noticing in a chapter otherwise full of long waits, since Joseph waited years in prison and decades before seeing his father again. Some prayers are answered at once, and some are answered slowly, and the difference is not a measure of how much they were heard.\n\nIt is also important to see what the answer looked like in practice. Their scheming was turned away from him, and the very next verse reports that he was sent to prison. The protection he received was not a comfortable rescue. He was preserved from sin, which is what he had actually asked for, while the difficulty continued.\n\nThis is one of the most valuable insights in the chapter. Allah answered by giving him precisely what he requested rather than what an observer might assume he needed. Joseph had said he preferred prison to disobedience, and that is what he was given.\n\n\"Surely He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing\" closes the verse with two names chosen for the situation. He hears the words of the one calling on Him, including a prayer spoken by a servant with no advocate in a foreign country. And He knows what lies behind the words: the pressure Joseph was under, the sincerity of his fear, and the danger he could not see.\n\nThe pairing of these names matters. Hearing alone might respond to what is said; knowledge understands what is meant. The response Joseph received addressed the request as he made it and the need as Allah knew it.",
  "35": "\"And so it occurred to those in charge, despite seeing all the proofs ˹of his innocence˺, that he should be imprisoned for a while\" delivers the outcome that Joseph had said he preferred, and the Quran states its injustice openly.\n\nThe phrase about the proofs is the sharpest part of the verse. Everyone involved knew he was innocent. The shirt torn from the back had settled it. The minister had told his wife the fault was hers. She herself had announced in front of the women of the city that she had pursued him and he had refused. There was no ambiguity whatsoever.\n\nHe was imprisoned anyway. The reason is not stated, and commentators have offered the obvious explanation: with the scandal now public, removing Joseph from view was the simplest way to quiet the talk and protect the household's reputation. A prison sentence for an innocent servant cost the powerful nothing.\n\nThis is a picture of institutional injustice that any reader can recognise. Nobody in the story disputes the facts. The decision is made for reasons of convenience, and the person with the least power carries the cost.\n\n\"For a while\" indicates a period of unspecified length. Nobody told him how long. He entered without a sentence to count down, which is its own kind of hardship, and the chapter later reveals that it lasted several years.\n\nThere is something remarkable in the sequence the Quran has just presented. Joseph prayed to be saved from sin, said he preferred prison, and was given prison. The answer to his prayer arrived in the form of a false imprisonment.\n\nFor anyone measuring their life by comfort, this looks like a failure. Measured by what Joseph asked for, it was a complete success. He kept his integrity, and the chapter goes on to show that the prison itself became the place where his rise began, since it was there that he met the man who would eventually mention his name to the king.",
  "36": "\"And two other servants went to jail with Joseph\" introduces the men whose dreams would eventually change his situation. They were servants of the king, and commentators mention that they had been accused of plotting against him, one connected with his drink and one with his food.\n\n\"One of them said, \"I dreamt I was pressing wine.\"\" and \"The other said, \"I dreamt I was carrying ˹some˺ bread on my head, from which birds were eating.\"\" give the two dreams. Both are short, strange, and closely tied to the men's former duties. One saw himself preparing drink; the other saw himself carrying bread that birds were consuming.\n\n\"˹Then both said,˺ \"Tell us their interpretation, for we surely see you as one of the good-doers.\"\" is the request, and their reason for asking is the important part.\n\nThey call him one who does good. This is how he had become known inside the prison, and it tells us how Joseph had been spending his time there. He had not withdrawn into bitterness. He had not spent years protesting his innocence to anyone who would listen. He had conducted himself in a way that made two fellow prisoners bring him their dreams because they trusted him.\n\nThe word they use is the same one the Quran applied to him earlier, when it said that wisdom and knowledge were given to him as the reward of those who do good. His character was consistent in the minister's house, in the moment of temptation, and now in a cell.\n\nThis detail carries a quiet lesson about circumstances. Joseph's position had collapsed twice, from favoured son to slave, and from trusted servant to prisoner. In each place he became the person others relied on. Where a person is placed is not usually in his control; how he behaves there is.\n\nThe interpretations he gives are accurate, as the chapter confirms, but before offering them he does something unexpected, which the following verses describe.",
  "37": "\"\"I can even tell you what kind of meal you will be served before you receive it.\" is Joseph's opening reply, and it is not a boast. He is establishing that what he is about to do is real, since two men in a cell have no reason to trust a fellow prisoner's claim to read dreams.\n\n\"This ˹knowledge˺ is from what my Lord has taught me.\" immediately redirects the credit. Before they can be impressed with him, he names the source. This is the pattern of the prophets throughout the Quran, and it is what separates a messenger from a fortune-teller. The gift is not his own talent, and he refuses to let it be received that way.\n\nThere is a practical wisdom in the sequence too. He has their attention now, and he uses it. Two men have come asking about their dreams, and Joseph recognises the moment for what it is: an opening to speak about something far more important than dreams.\n\n\"I have shunned the faith of a people who disbelieve in Allah and deny the Hereafter.\" describes the position he has taken. The Arabic indicates leaving something behind deliberately, abandoning it. He is telling them plainly that he does not share the religion of Egypt, and he identifies its two central failures: rejecting Allah and denying the life to come.\n\nConsider his situation while saying this. He is a foreign slave, imprisoned without cause, entirely dependent on the goodwill of the authorities who hold him. Openly criticising the national religion is not a safe activity for someone in that position. He does it anyway, calmly, in the course of answering a question about dreams.\n\nWhat is instructive is his method. He does not lecture them unprompted, and he does not withhold the help they came for. He answers, he credits Allah, and he explains his own faith clearly before returning to their question. The invitation is woven into an ordinary act of service rather than delivered as a confrontation.",
  "38": "\"\"I follow the faith of my fathers: Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.\" continues Joseph's explanation and gives his religion a lineage. He is not presenting a personal philosophy invented in a prison cell. He is naming three men, and the two prisoners would have understood immediately that this was an established tradition with a history behind it.\n\nThere is also something quietly powerful in this for Joseph himself. He is a slave in Egypt, stripped of everything, and the identity he claims is that of his great-grandfather, grandfather, and father. Prison had taken his freedom and not his sense of who he was.\n\nIt is worth noticing that following the faith of his fathers is presented positively here, while elsewhere in the Quran people are criticised for clinging to what their forefathers worshipped. The difference is not inconsistency. What Joseph inherited was revelation from Allah, transmitted through prophets and confirmed by evidence. What the deniers inherited was invention with nothing behind it. Inheritance is valuable when it carries truth and worthless when it carries only habit.\n\n\"It is not ˹right˺ for us to associate anything with Allah ˹in worship˺.\" states the core of that faith in a single line. The Arabic indicates that it is not fitting or possible for people like them to give any share of worship to something beside Allah. He is speaking in a land where the gods were many and the king himself was treated as divine, and he draws the line without hesitation.\n\n\"This is part of Allah's grace upon us and humanity\" reframes monotheism as a gift rather than a burden. Knowing who deserves worship is described as a favour, given to the prophets and offered to everyone.\n\n\"But most people are not grateful\" identifies the usual response. The gift is available, and most people do not recognise it as one. This connects to a theme running through the surrounding chapters, where knowledge, provision, and guidance are all described as favours that the majority overlook.",
  "39": "\"\"O my fellow-prisoners!\" is how Joseph addresses them, and the phrase deserves a moment. He calls them companions of the prison, not servants of the king or accused criminals. He places himself alongside them, sharing their situation rather than speaking down from any imagined height.\n\nThat form of address does something important before the argument even begins. People rarely accept correction from someone who is talking at them from a distance. Joseph is in the same cell, eating the same food, waiting for the same uncertain outcome, and he speaks accordingly.\n\n\"Which is far better: many different lords or Allah—the One, the Supreme?\" is the question itself, and it is posed as a comparison rather than as an accusation. He does not tell them their religion is contemptible. He invites them to weigh two options side by side.\n\nThe Arabic word for these many lords indicates masters who are scattered and separate, pulling in different directions. This was not an abstract idea for two Egyptian servants. They had lived under a system of many gods with competing domains, and they had also lived under a hierarchy of human masters. They knew what it was like to serve several authorities whose demands did not agree.\n\nAnyone who has worked for several bosses at once understands the argument immediately. Divided authority produces confusion, contradictory orders, and a servant who can never satisfy everyone. The Quran makes this comparison explicit elsewhere, in Surah az-Zumar, where a man owned by several quarrelling partners is contrasted with a man belonging wholly to one master.\n\nThe two names Joseph uses for Allah answer the alternative precisely. \"Al-Wahid\" means the One, unique with no partner, which removes the confusion of scattered lords. \"Al-Qahhar\" means the Irresistible, the One who prevails over everything, which removes any suggestion that other powers could compete with Him.\n\nTwo men in a cell, powerless before their own king, are being told that the real Authority is single and cannot be overcome.",
  "40": "\"\"Whatever ˹idols˺ you worship instead of Him are mere names which you and your forefathers have made up\" delivers the argument that follows Joseph's question. He has asked which is better; now he explains what the alternative actually consists of.\n\nThe claim is precise and devastating. These gods are names. Not beings with power that people have misjudged, but labels invented by human beings and then treated as realities. Someone chose a name, attached it to a stone or a concept, and later generations inherited the name and forgot that it had been assigned.\n\n\"A practice Allah has never authorized\" removes the only defence that could have been offered. If revelation had sanctioned these deities, there would be something to discuss. There is no such sanction, so the entire structure rests on human invention, which is the same charge made throughout the Quran against every false religion.\n\n\"It is only Allah Who decides\" states the principle behind all of it. The Arabic indicates that judgement and authority belong to Him alone. This covers both the ultimate decision about people's fates and the authority to determine what is right, lawful, and required. A god who cannot decide anything is not a god, and no created thing holds that authority.\n\n\"He has commanded that you worship none but Him\" gives the practical conclusion, and it is the same instruction every prophet in the preceding chapters delivered to his people.\n\n\"That is the upright faith\" names the result. The Arabic term indicates the religion that stands straight, firm and correct, without the crookedness that comes from invention.\n\n\"But most people do not know\" ends where several statements in these chapters end. The information is available and the reasoning is simple, and the majority still do not arrive at it.\n\nTwo prisoners had asked about their dreams. What they received first was a complete case for the oneness of Allah, delivered by a man whose own life offered no visible evidence that serving Him produced worldly reward.",
  "41": "\"\"O my fellow-prisoners! ˹The first˺ one of you will serve wine to his master\" begins the answer they had originally asked for. Joseph has spoken about Allah, and now he returns to their dreams and interprets them accurately.\n\nThe first man's dream of pressing wine meant restoration to his former duty. He would be released and would return to serving the king's drink, exactly as before.\n\n\"And the other will be crucified and the birds will eat from his head\" gives the second interpretation, and it is a death sentence. The dream of carrying bread that birds were eating from meant that his own body would be exposed and the birds would take from it.\n\nDelivering this must have been extremely difficult. A man in a cell asks a fellow prisoner a friendly question, and the honest answer is that he will be executed. Joseph does not soften it, evade it, or find a comforting way to phrase it. Nor does he refuse to answer.\n\nCommentators have discussed whether he should have withheld such news, and the text gives its own reply. Truth given honestly allows a person to prepare, and there is a possibility that the man might have used his remaining time well. False comfort would have taken even that from him.\n\n\"The matter about which you inquired has been decided.\" closes the interpretation with finality. The Arabic indicates that the affair they asked about has been settled and determined. There is nothing further to discuss, no alternative reading to seek, no second opinion that will change it.\n\nNotice the balance of the two outcomes. One man receives good news and one receives the worst possible news, from the same interpreter, in the same breath. Joseph does not adjust his answers to be liked, and this is precisely what makes him trustworthy.\n\nHis own position remains unchanged throughout. He is still imprisoned without cause, still with no release date, and he has just told one man that he is going home.",
  "42": "\"Then he said to the one he knew would survive, \"Mention me in the presence of your master.\"\" is Joseph taking a practical step toward his own freedom. He had a fellow prisoner about to be released into the king's service, and he asked him to raise his case.\n\nThis deserves emphasis, because people sometimes imagine that trust in Allah means refusing to act. Joseph had already shown complete reliance on his Lord, praying in the moment of temptation and accepting prison rather than sin. That reliance did not stop him from using a legitimate means when one appeared. Asking someone to speak for you is entirely ordinary and entirely permitted.\n\n\"But Satan made him forget to mention Joseph to his master\" reports what happened instead. The released man walked out, returned to his comfortable position, and the promise slipped from his mind.\n\nThere is a question here that commentators discussed at length. Who forgot: the cupbearer or Joseph? The plain reading, held by most scholars, is that the released servant forgot. Some early commentators suggested a different reading in which Joseph was caused to forget his Lord for a moment by relying on a human being, and reports about this exist in the tafsir literature. Since the Quran's wording most naturally points to the man who left, and since Joseph's reliance on Allah is affirmed everywhere in this chapter, the first reading is the sounder one.\n\n\"So he remained in prison for several years\" states the cost. Commentators mention various lengths, commonly around seven years, and the Quran leaves it unspecified.\n\nThe lesson in this is hard and worth stating plainly. Joseph did the right thing, used a lawful means, and the means failed. This happens constantly in ordinary life: an application is forgotten, a promise is not kept, an opportunity evaporates through someone else's carelessness. The chapter shows that such failures are not accidents in the larger arrangement, since the delay placed Joseph in prison at exactly the moment the king would need him.",
  "43": "\"And ˹one day˺ the King said, \"I dreamt of seven fat cows eaten up by seven skinny ones; and seven green ears of grain and ˹seven˺ others dry.\"\" moves the story into the palace, years after the previous verse. The forgotten prisoner is still forgotten, and the events that will free him begin somewhere he cannot see.\n\nThe dream is agricultural, which suits a country whose entire life depended on the Nile and its harvests. Fat cattle and green grain meant abundance; thin cattle and dried grain meant scarcity. The image of the thin consuming the fat suggests that the years of shortage would swallow up the years of plenty.\n\nNote the title used here. The Quran calls the Egyptian ruler of Joseph's time \"al-malik,\" the king, while it consistently calls the ruler in Moses' time Pharaoh. This distinction is historically interesting, since the title Pharaoh came into use for Egyptian rulers in a later period than the one usually associated with Joseph. The Quran's precision on a point of this kind, in a text delivered in seventh-century Arabia, is worth noting.\n\n\"O chiefs! Tell me the meaning of my dream if you can interpret dreams.\" shows the king taking his dream seriously and calling on his advisers.\n\nHis seriousness is significant. Many rulers would dismiss a strange dream, and this one recognised that something had been shown to him. The Quran distinguishes between meaningless dreams and true ones, and the Prophet Muhammad said in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim that a good dream is from Allah while a disturbing one comes from Satan.\n\nBehind all of this, the arrangement running through the whole chapter is visible again. A dream sent to a man who could not interpret it, in a court with no one able to help, at exactly the moment when a forgotten prisoner possessed precisely that skill.",
  "44": "\"\"These are confused visions and we do not know the interpretation of such dreams.\" is the reply of the king's advisers, and it is an admission of failure dressed up as an explanation.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates jumbled dreams, the disordered images that come from a troubled mind rather than from anything meaningful. They are telling the king that his dream means nothing, which conveniently removes any obligation to interpret it.\n\nNotice the structure of their answer. First they classify the dream as nonsense, then they admit they cannot interpret such things. If it were genuinely meaningless, the second part would be unnecessary. Their assessment came from their inability rather than from any examination of the dream itself.\n\nThis is a recognisable habit. People frequently dismiss what they cannot handle, declaring a question unanswerable or a problem imaginary rather than admitting a limit. There is more honesty in the second half of their sentence than in the first.\n\nThese were the professionals of the court, the men whose position depended on advising the king, including on matters of dreams and omens. Egypt had a substantial class of such specialists. Faced with a genuine case, they had nothing.\n\nThe contrast the chapter is building is deliberate. The experts of the empire, with their training and their status, could not read a dream. A foreign prisoner, forgotten in a cell, could read it immediately and had already proved it years earlier with the two servants.\n\nThere is a further point in their failure. Had they produced a confident but wrong interpretation, Egypt might have prepared for the wrong future entirely, and the famine would have destroyed the country. Their honest incapacity left the door open for someone who actually knew.\n\nThat door is about to be opened by a man who had every reason never to think of Joseph again, and who suddenly remembers a promise he made years ago in a prison cell.",
  "45": "\"˹Finally,˺ the surviving ex-prisoner remembered ˹Joseph˺ after a long time\" is the moment the door opens. The man who had walked out of prison years earlier, carrying a request he never delivered, suddenly recalls it in the middle of a royal crisis.\n\nThe wording notes how much time had passed. Whatever the exact number of years, it was long enough for Joseph's request to have been completely buried under an ordinary life. The memory returns not through guilt but through circumstance: he is standing in a room where nobody can interpret a dream, and he knows someone who can.\n\nThere is something worth pausing over in the timing. Had he remembered immediately, Joseph would probably have been released quietly as a favour, returning to obscurity as a freed servant with no position. Because he forgot, the recollection came at the one moment when Egypt urgently needed what Joseph had, and the result was authority over the whole country's food supply.\n\nThe chapter has already prepared the reader for this pattern. The pit, the caravan, the cheap sale, the false accusation, the prison sentence: each apparent setback was a step. The forgetting was another.\n\nThis does not make the man's negligence admirable. He broke a promise to someone who had helped him, and a man sat in a cell for years partly because of it. The Quran assigns that forgetting to Satan's interference in the earlier verse. Human failure remains failure even when Allah weaves it into something good.\n\n\"And said, \"I will tell you its interpretation, so send me forth ˹to Joseph˺.\"\" is his offer to the court. Notice that he does not claim the skill himself, which he easily could have done. He asks to be sent to the person who has it.\n\nHe also does not name Joseph as a wrongly imprisoned man or explain the situation. He simply asks for permission to go, which suggests he understood that the fastest route to helping was to produce the answer rather than to argue a case.",
  "46": "\"\"Joseph, O man of truth!\" is how the messenger addresses him on entering the prison, and the title tells us how Joseph was remembered. Years had passed, and what stayed in this man's mind was not Joseph's appearance or his misfortune but his truthfulness.\n\nThe Arabic term indicates someone deeply and consistently truthful, whose honesty is a settled quality rather than an occasional performance. In the Quran this word is used of the highest ranks of the righteous, and its use here comes from a man of the Egyptian court who had known Joseph only as a fellow prisoner.\n\nThere is something instructive in that. Joseph had no platform, no freedom, and no way to build a reputation. What he had was consistent conduct in a cell, and it left an impression that survived years of silence.\n\n\"Interpret for us ˹the dream of˺ seven fat cows eaten up by seven skinny ones; and seven green ears of grain and ˹seven˺ others dry\" repeats the king's dream word for word. The messenger relays it exactly as it was described, which matters, since an accurate interpretation depends on accurate reporting.\n\n\"So that I may return to the people and let them know\" states his purpose. He intends to carry the answer back to the court.\n\nWhat is striking is what the message does not include. There is no apology for the broken promise, no mention of Joseph's imprisonment, and no offer of release in exchange for the interpretation. The man asks for help without addressing the wrong that lies between them.\n\nJoseph's response, which follows immediately, contains no reproach either. He does not say that he has waited years for this visit, and he does not make his answer conditional on being freed first. He gives the interpretation, and he gives more than was asked for.\n\nThat response says as much about him as the title the messenger used.",
  "47": "\"\"You will plant ˹grain˺ for seven consecutive years\" begins Joseph's interpretation, and he starts without any preamble. No complaint about his imprisonment, no negotiation, no reminder of the promise that was forgotten. A question was asked and he answers it.\n\nThe seven fat cows and seven green ears are read as seven years of good harvests. The reading is confident and specific: continuous, uninterrupted cultivation for seven seasons.\n\n\"Leaving in the ear whatever you will harvest, except for the little you will eat\" is the part nobody asked for. This is not interpretation at all; it is policy advice. Joseph does not merely tell them what the dream means, he tells them what to do about it.\n\nThe instruction is practically brilliant. Grain left in the ear stores far better than threshed grain, resisting insects and moisture. This is agricultural knowledge of a high order, and it shows that Joseph's gift was not limited to reading dreams. The chapter had said earlier that he was given wisdom and knowledge, and this is what that combination looks like in application.\n\nThe additional advice about eating only a little establishes a discipline of restraint during years of abundance, which is the hardest time to practise it. When harvests are good, prices fall, storage feels unnecessary, and consumption rises naturally. Telling a nation to live modestly during plenty requires someone who can see past the present season.\n\nThere is a broader principle here that applies well beyond ancient Egypt. Prosperity is the time to prepare for scarcity, and the failure to do so is one of the most common patterns in both personal finance and national policy. People save when they are frightened, which is precisely when saving is most difficult.\n\nJoseph offers all of this from a prison cell, for a country that had wronged him, to a court that had forgotten him. He simply gives the best answer he can.",
  "48": "\"\"Then after that will come seven years of great hardship\" continues the interpretation with the second half of the dream. The seven thin cows and dried ears are read as seven years of severe famine, and the Arabic word indicates years that are harsh and difficult.\n\n\"Which will consume whatever you have saved\" explains the image of the thin cattle devouring the fat ones. The famine years would eat through the stored surplus of the good years. This is exactly how the dream had pictured it, and Joseph's reading connects the symbol to the reality precisely.\n\nThe phrase also carries a warning about the scale of what was coming. The stores would not merely be reduced; they would be consumed. Seven years of surplus would be entirely absorbed by seven years of shortage, which tells the court how complete the preparation had to be. Anything less than a full seven years of saving would leave the country short.\n\n\"Except the little you will store ˹for seed˺\" adds a further piece of practical guidance. Something must be held back beyond consumption, because a nation that eats its entire seed stock during famine cannot plant when the rains return. Survival is not enough; recovery has to be protected.\n\nThis detail again shows the difference between interpreting a dream and understanding a situation. Nobody asked Joseph how to manage a famine. He saw what the dream implied and supplied what the country would need.\n\nThere is a moral dimension to this too, easily overlooked. Joseph is giving careful, generous advice to the government that imprisoned him without cause. He could have given a bare interpretation and left them to work out the consequences. Instead he provides a plan that would save the lives of the people who wronged him, and, as the chapter later reveals, would also save his own family and the surrounding region.\n\nThe advice was offered before any release was mentioned, and Joseph had no assurance that it would benefit him at all.",
  "49": "\"\"Then after that will come a year in which people will receive abundant rain and they will press ˹oil and wine˺.\" adds something remarkable: information that was not in the dream at all.\n\nThe king had seen fourteen symbols, seven fat and seven thin, and Joseph has explained all of them. This final year comes from somewhere else. Commentators note that this is knowledge given to him by Allah beyond the dream's content, which fits what he told the two prisoners years earlier, that his knowledge came from what his Lord had taught him.\n\nConsider what this addition accomplishes. A nation told only about seven good years followed by seven terrible ones would be facing an ending with no horizon beyond it. Joseph gives them a future. After the famine ends, rain returns, crops recover, and life resumes in abundance. The mention of pressing indicates olives and grapes, which are the produce of settled prosperity rather than desperate survival.\n\nThere is a pastoral wisdom in this that goes beyond agriculture. Warnings are far easier to bear when they include an end point. People can endure a hardship whose limit they know, and this is why the Quran so often pairs its warnings with hope, and why Joseph's interpretation does not stop at the worst part.\n\nThe detail also served a practical purpose. A government planning only for survival might have made different decisions than one planning for recovery, and knowing that the fourteenth year would bring rain shapes how much seed to protect and how much despair to allow.\n\nFor the messenger standing in the cell, this was a complete answer, delivered in three verses by a man with nothing to gain. He had asked for an interpretation and received a fourteen-year national strategy with an outcome attached.\n\nHe returns to the court, and the king's reaction in the next verse is immediate. What is unexpected is Joseph's response to the summons.",
  "50": "\"\"The King ˹then˺ said, \"Bring him to me.\"\" is the natural reaction of a ruler who has just received the answer his entire court could not produce. The command is simple: fetch this man.\n\nFor anyone imprisoned unjustly for years, this is the moment of release. Most people would run through the open door without a backward glance.\n\n\"Joseph said, \"Go back to your master and ask him about the case of the women who cut their hands.\"\" is his response, and it is one of the most extraordinary moments in the chapter. He refuses to leave.\n\nHe is not being difficult, and he is not bargaining for a position. He is asking for an investigation. Joseph had been imprisoned under a cloud of scandal, and everyone in the palace circle knew the rumour. Walking out through a royal favour would have freed his body while leaving his name exactly where it was: the servant who tried to violate his master's wife, released because he happened to be useful.\n\nHe wanted the record corrected first. Freedom without honour was not what he had waited for.\n\nThere is real courage in this. He was still a prisoner with no power, making a demand of a king who had merely summoned him. The request could have irritated the court and ended the whole opportunity.\n\nIt is also worth noticing how he frames it. He does not accuse the minister's wife by name, and he does not describe what she did. He simply asks about the case of the women who cut their hands, letting the facts be discovered rather than asserting them.\n\n\"Surely my Lord has ˹full˺ knowledge of their cunning.\" closes his message by placing the matter where it always rested. Whatever the court concluded, Allah already knew the truth. Joseph asks for an inquiry among people while affirming that the real record was never in doubt.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad is reported to have expressed admiration for this patience, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, remarking that had he been in Joseph's place, he would have hastened to accept the offer.",
  "51": "\"\"The King asked ˹the women˺, \"What did you get when you tried to seduce Joseph?\"\" shows the investigation being opened. Joseph's request was granted, and the king questions the women directly rather than dismissing the matter.\n\nThe phrasing of the question is careful. He does not ask whether Joseph attacked anyone; he asks about their attempt on him. This suggests the truth was already suspected, and it gave the women little room to construct an alternative story.\n\n\"They replied, \"Allah forbid! We know nothing indecent about him.\"\" is their answer, and it is a complete clearing of his name. These were the same women who had gossiped about the scandal, then joined in pressuring him. Under formal questioning, every one of them testifies to his innocence.\n\n\"Then the Chief Minister's wife admitted, \"Now the truth has come to light.\"\" is the decisive testimony. The Arabic phrase indicates that the truth has now become clear and undeniable. She is acknowledging that a fact everyone had buried has surfaced.\n\n\"It was I who tried to seduce him, and he is surely truthful.\" is a full confession. She names herself as the one who pursued him and declares that he told the truth all along.\n\nThis is what Joseph had waited for, and it is why he refused to leave the prison without it. Had he walked out earlier, this statement would never have been made. The confession came only because he insisted on the inquiry.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the timing of her admission. Years had passed. Joseph was in prison and powerless, and she had every opportunity to stay silent. What appears to have moved her was the discovery that the king himself was asking, and perhaps the weight of a lie carried too long. Her words in the following verses suggest genuine remorse rather than mere exposure.\n\nFor Joseph, the sequence was now complete: refusal, false accusation, imprisonment, patience, and finally a public vindication from the only mouth that could deliver it.",
  "52": "\"\"From this, Joseph should know that I did not speak dishonestly about him in his absence\" continues the minister's wife's statement, and it explains why she confessed.\n\nCommentators have differed over who is speaking these words. Most read them as a continuation of her speech, in which she wants Joseph to know that when the matter was investigated in his absence, she did not repeat the lie. A minority understood the words as Joseph's, reflecting on why he had asked for the inquiry before leaving the prison. The first reading follows the flow of the passage more naturally, and it is the one most commentators adopted.\n\nRead as her words, the statement is a recognition that a wrong done in someone's absence must be corrected in their absence. Joseph was not in the room. He could not hear what was said, defend himself, or know what verdict was reached. She could have softened her account or shared the blame, and he would never have known the difference.\n\nThe Arabic indicates betraying someone secretly, behind their back. This is a precise description of what backbiting and false accusation actually are: harm done to a person who is not present to answer.\n\n\"For Allah certainly does not guide the scheming of the dishonest.\" gives the reasoning behind her change of course. The Arabic word for scheming is the same one used earlier in this chapter for the plots against Joseph, and the statement is a general principle: plans built on treachery are not brought to success by Allah.\n\nHer own experience proved it. Her scheme had achieved its immediate goal, since Joseph went to prison and the scandal was contained. Years later the plan had unravelled completely, and she was standing before the king admitting everything.\n\nThis is the same truth Joseph himself had spoken in her house when he refused her, saying that the wrongdoers never succeed. She is now confirming it from the other side.",
  "53": "\"\"And I do not seek to free myself from blame\" is a striking line, and it deserves careful reading. Having just confessed, the speaker refuses to present herself as innocent or to excuse what she did by any circumstance.\n\nAs with the previous verse, commentators differed over the speaker. Many read this as the continuation of the minister's wife's confession, which is the natural flow of the passage. Some read it as Joseph speaking about himself, expressing humility after his vindication. Both readings have been held by respected scholars, and both produce sound meanings.\n\n\"For indeed the soul is ever inclined to evil\" gives the reason for that refusal to self-justify. The Arabic phrase refers to the self that persistently commands what is wrong, pushing a person toward what he desires rather than what is right.\n\nIslamic scholars have long discussed the different states of the human self described in the Quran. This one, the self that commands evil, is the untrained state, in which appetite gives the orders. Elsewhere the Quran mentions the self that blames itself, which is the state of a person whose conscience has become active, and the soul at peace, which is the state of one who has been settled by faith.\n\nThis is an honest and unflattering picture of human nature, and it explains a great deal about the story that has just been told. Ten brothers who threw a boy into a well, a woman who imprisoned an innocent man, a court that knew he was innocent and confined him anyway: none of them were monsters. They were people following what their selves wanted.\n\n\"Except those shown mercy by my Lord\" names the only exception. Nobody rises above this by willpower alone. Protection is granted, which is exactly how the chapter described Joseph's own escape from temptation.\n\n\"Surely my Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" closes with the door left open. A person who has just admitted serious wrongdoing ends by naming the two attributes that make repentance worth attempting.",
  "54": "\"\"Bring him to me. I will employ him exclusively in my service.\" is the king's second summons, and the difference from the first is significant. Earlier he had simply asked for Joseph to be brought. Now, after the investigation has cleared him, the king announces his intention to attach Joseph to himself personally.\n\nThe Arabic indicates making him his own, reserving him for his own service and confidence. This is a promotion offered before the two men have even met.\n\n\"And when Joseph spoke to him, the King said, \"Today you are highly esteemed and fully trusted by us.\"\" describes their first conversation and its immediate effect. The Quran does not record what was said. It records only that Joseph spoke and that the king's verdict followed at once.\n\nTwo qualities are named. The first is high standing, indicating rank and honour in the king's eyes. The second is trustworthiness, which is the more remarkable of the two. Trust of that kind is usually earned over years, and the king extends it after a single meeting.\n\nSomething in Joseph's speech produced this. A man who had been a slave and a prisoner for the better part of two decades walked into a royal audience and left it with the highest confidence a ruler can offer.\n\nNotice the sequence the chapter has followed. Joseph refused to be released until his innocence was established. Because he waited, he entered the palace not as a pardoned convict but as a man publicly cleared by testimony. The position he was offered rested on a reputation that had been repaired first.\n\nThere is a practical observation here about integrity over time. Joseph had nothing to hide, and this is what allowed him to insist on an inquiry. A person carrying secrets cannot afford investigation, and so can never be fully cleared, however useful he becomes.\n\nWhat he asks for in the next verse is not comfort or compensation, which is itself revealing.",
  "55": "\"\"Put me in charge of the store-houses of the land\" is Joseph's request, and it is not what most people in his position would have asked for.\n\nHe had just been offered a place of honour and trust beside the king. He could have requested freedom, wealth, safe passage home to his father, or compensation for years of wrongful imprisonment. Instead he asks for the hardest job in Egypt: responsibility for the nation's food supply during fourteen years of feast and famine.\n\nThis is a request for work, not for reward. He had interpreted the dream and knew exactly what was coming. Seven years of disciplined saving followed by seven years of managing scarcity for an entire population is a burden that would exhaust anyone. He asked for it because he understood the situation and knew he could handle it.\n\nScholars have discussed whether asking for a position of authority is permissible, since the Prophet advised against seeking such posts and told a companion, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim, not to ask for leadership, because if it is given after asking he will be left to it. The general guidance discourages ambition for office. What distinguishes Joseph's case, as commentators have noted, is that he was uniquely qualified, the need was urgent, and no one else could do it. Seeking a position to serve a genuine need is not the same as seeking one for status.\n\n\"For I am truly reliable and adept.\" explains why he is suitable, and he says it plainly. This is not boasting; it is an accurate statement of qualification, which the situation required.\n\nBoth qualities are necessary and neither is sufficient alone. A trustworthy person without competence will ruin the work sincerely. A competent person without integrity will manage the stores brilliantly and steal from them. Joseph offers both, and his life had already demonstrated each: honesty when nobody was watching in a locked house, and knowledge that the entire royal court lacked.",
  "56": "\"This is how We established Joseph in the land to settle wherever he pleased\" gives the divine perspective on everything that has just happened, and the wording deliberately echoes an earlier verse. When Joseph was first bought as a slave, the Quran said in almost identical terms that this was how he was established in the land.\n\nThe repetition invites comparison. The first establishment placed him in an Egyptian household as purchased property. The second places him in authority over the country's resources. Both are described with the same phrase, which suggests that the first was not a detour on the way to the second but part of the same act.\n\n\"To settle wherever he pleased\" describes the freedom he now had, and it lands with particular force for a man who had spent years in a cell with no release date and before that in a house he could not leave.\n\n\"We shower Our mercy on whoever We will\" places the outcome where it belongs. Joseph's rise was not the reward of his cleverness, though he was extraordinarily capable, nor the result of good fortune. It is described as mercy, given according to Allah's will.\n\n\"And We never discount the reward of the good-doers\" completes the statement with a principle stated elsewhere in these chapters as well. The Arabic verb indicates causing something to be wasted or lost, and its denial is absolute.\n\nThis is the answer to a question the chapter has been raising for fifty verses. Joseph did good consistently: as a boy who kept his father's advice, as a servant who refused betrayal, as a prisoner who taught his companions and interpreted honestly, as an adviser who gave a nation his best counsel while it kept him locked up. For most of that time none of it produced any visible return.\n\nThe verse says nothing was lost. The reward existed throughout the years when it could not be seen, which is precisely when believing this is hardest and most necessary.",
  "57": "\"And the reward of the Hereafter is far better for those who are faithful and are mindful ˹of Allah˺\" adds a corrective to everything the previous verse described, and its placement is deliberate.\n\nJoseph has just been given authority over Egypt, freedom of movement, and the trust of a king. Any listener following the story would naturally feel that the account has reached its happy conclusion. The verse interrupts that feeling by pointing past it.\n\nWhatever Joseph received in Egypt, and it was considerable, is described as inferior to what awaits. The Arabic indicates that the reward of the next life is better, without qualification.\n\nThere is a reason the Quran inserts this here rather than at the end of the chapter. Stories of worldly success can teach the wrong lesson. A reader might conclude that patience and integrity are strategies for eventual promotion, and that the point of Joseph's endurance was the position he finally obtained. That reading would be a mistake, and the verse prevents it.\n\nConsider the implication. Many righteous people never receive an Egyptian ministry. Prophets were killed, believers were tortured, and this chapter's own audience in Mecca was living under boycott with no rescue in sight. If the promise depended on worldly reversal, most of them would have nothing.\n\nThe two conditions named are the same pair the Quran uses throughout: belief and mindfulness of Allah. The Arabic \"taqwa\" describes an awareness of Allah that makes a person careful about what he does. These were exactly Joseph's qualities in the locked room, when no worldly reward was in view and no one would have known.\n\nThe verse also quietly explains Joseph's own conduct. A man who genuinely holds this belief can refuse a powerful woman, prefer a prison cell, and give honest advice to a court that wronged him, because he is not calculating in the currency that everyone around him is using.",
  "58": "\"And Joseph's brothers came and entered his presence\" resumes the narrative after a jump of many years. The famine has begun, and it has reached beyond Egypt into the surrounding lands, including Canaan where Jacob's family lived. People came to Egypt because Egypt had grain, and Egypt had grain because of the man they were about to stand in front of.\n\nThe brothers appear as ordinary petitioners in a queue of foreigners seeking food. They had travelled a considerable distance out of need, which is a reversal in itself: the men who once held power over a helpless boy now stand as supplicants.\n\n\"He recognized them but they were unaware of who he really was.\" is the sentence the chapter has been building toward since the well. Joseph knew them instantly. They had been grown men when he last saw them, and their appearance had changed little in the intervening years.\n\nThey did not know him. He had been a boy of perhaps twelve or thirteen, and now he was a man in his thirties or forties, dressed as an Egyptian official, speaking as the head of the country's food administration. Nothing about him suggested the brother they had thrown into a pit. Most of all, they had no reason to imagine he was alive, let alone here.\n\nThis is exactly what Joseph had been told at the bottom of the well, when Allah inspired him that a day would come when he would remind them of what they did while they were unaware of who he was. Decades later the promise arrives precisely as it was described.\n\nWhat happens in this moment is entirely internal, and the Quran does not describe Joseph's feelings. He says nothing, reveals nothing, and continues with the business of distributing grain. Whatever he felt on seeing the faces of the men who ended his childhood, he kept it to himself and thought carefully about what to do next.",
  "59": "\"When he had provided them with their supplies\" shows Joseph doing his job before doing anything else. The men who threw him into a well received their full allocation of grain, measured out properly, exactly like every other family that came.\n\nThis detail is easy to pass over and worth dwelling on. He had complete power over them and complete anonymity. He could have refused them, delayed them, or quietly given them less. He gave them what they came for first, and only then began to arrange what he needed.\n\n\"He demanded, \"Bring me your brother on your father's side.\"\" introduces his plan. He asks for Benjamin, his only full brother, the one who had been left at home. Joseph presumably learned during their conversation about the family's situation, and he now wants to see the brother he had not seen since childhood.\n\nThe request would have seemed unremarkable to them. An official checking the accuracy of a household's claim before issuing a larger allocation is ordinary administration.\n\n\"Do you not see that I give full measure and I am the best of hosts?\" is how he justifies the request, and it also reveals how he was running the distribution. He gave full measure, which in a famine, when officials everywhere skim from desperate people, is a striking claim. And he treated arrivals hospitably.\n\nThere is a connection here worth noticing. Joseph's demand for honest measure recalls the standard the Quran gives to merchants elsewhere, and it shows that his integrity as a servant and a prisoner carried into his conduct as a minister. Power did not change him.\n\nHis words are also carefully chosen to build trust. He is telling them that they have been dealt with generously, which makes his condition sound reasonable rather than threatening. The pressure comes in the next verse, and it comes without insult or cruelty.",
  "60": "\"\"But if you do not bring him to me ˹next time˺, I will have no grain for you\"\" applies the pressure Joseph needed. It is a straightforward condition: no brother, no supplies.\n\nThe weight of this can only be understood in context. There was a famine across the region, Egypt had the grain, and this man controlled it. He was not threatening violence. He was simply declining future business, which in that situation amounted to the same thing for a family that could not feed itself.\n\n\"Nor will you ever come close to me again.\" closes the other route. They would not be able to appeal, negotiate, or seek a different official. The matter would be settled permanently.\n\nIt is worth asking whether this was fair. Joseph is using his position to compel a family to bring him a particular person, and the brothers had no idea why. Commentators have generally understood his motive as legitimate: he wanted to see Benjamin, to assess whether the brothers had treated him as they treated Joseph, and eventually to reunite the family. The chapter's later verses confirm that his purpose was reunion rather than revenge, and Allah is described as guiding his plan.\n\nNotice also the restraint in what he does not do. He has the power to arrest them, to confront them with their crime, or to punish them. Instead he uses the minimum leverage necessary to bring his brother to Egypt.\n\nThere is something else in this exchange that the brothers could not perceive. The man setting conditions on them was doing so out of longing rather than hostility. He wanted his family, and this was the only way he could see to reach them without revealing himself before he was ready.\n\nFor a listener in Mecca, the scene had its own resonance. The brothers stood in front of the person they had wronged, entirely at his mercy, and he was arranging to save them.",
  "61": "\"\"We will try to convince his father to let him come. We will certainly do ˹our best˺.\" is the brothers' reply, and the way they phrase it is revealing.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that they will attempt to persuade his father, working on him to release the boy. Notice the wording: they say \"his father,\" not \"our father.\" Commentators have observed this, and one reasonable reading is that they are speaking to the Egyptian official about the household situation as outsiders would describe it. Another reading is that the phrase quietly acknowledges the special relationship between Jacob and Benjamin, the same closeness they had resented in the case of Joseph.\n\nTheir promise to do their best is a realistic answer rather than a confident one. They know their father, and they know how difficult this request will be. The last time they took a favoured son out with them, he did not come back. Whatever explanation they gave at the time, Jacob had never accepted it.\n\nThere is something quietly uncomfortable in their position. They are being asked to persuade an old man to hand over his remaining beloved son, and the only reason he would hesitate is the crime they committed decades earlier. The obstacle they now face is entirely of their own making.\n\nThis is one of the ways wrongdoing continues to cost long after the act. Trust broken in the past becomes an obstacle in situations no one anticipated. They could not simply say that Benjamin would be safe, because their word on exactly that subject had already proved worthless.\n\nTheir difficulty is also part of what makes Joseph's plan work. He needed them to bring Benjamin, and the tension of asking their father was going to force the family's old wound into the open. The conversation that follows in the next verses is the first time in the chapter that Jacob speaks directly about what he suspects.\n\nFor now they leave Egypt with their grain, unaware that the official has already arranged something else in their bags.",
  "62": "\"Joseph ordered his servants to put his brothers' money back into their saddlebags\" describes a quiet act carried out without their knowledge. The payment they had made for the grain was returned, hidden among their supplies, to be discovered only when they reached home.\n\nCommentators have offered several explanations for this, and they complement one another. It was generosity toward a family he knew to be struggling in a famine. It was also practical, since a household with limited resources might not be able to afford a second journey, and Joseph needed them to return.\n\n\"So that they would find it when they returned to their family\" indicates the intended timing. The discovery would happen in front of everyone at home, including his father, which added weight to their account of the generous Egyptian official.\n\n\"And perhaps they would come back\" states the purpose plainly. Everything Joseph does in this section is designed to bring them back with Benjamin.\n\nWhat is notable is the manner of the giving. He does not announce it, take credit for it, or use it to place them under obligation while they stand before him. They receive it after they have left, from someone they cannot thank.\n\nThere is a principle in Islamic teaching that charity given quietly is superior, and the Prophet mentioned among those who will be shaded on the Day of Judgment a man who gives in charity so secretly that his left hand does not know what his right hand has given, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim. Joseph's arrangement fits that description exactly, though his motive included a purpose of his own.\n\nThe act also carried a risk he must have weighed. Returned money could have alarmed them, suggesting a trap or an accusation of theft, and their reaction when they discover it, described a few verses later, shows they were indeed uncertain how to read it. Joseph judged that the pull of generosity and need would outweigh the suspicion.",
  "63": "\"When Joseph's brothers returned to their father, they pleaded, \"O our father! We have been denied ˹further˺ supplies.\"\" begins the difficult conversation they had promised to attempt. They open with the consequence rather than the request, which is a careful way to start.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that the measure of grain has been withheld from them for the future. They present it as something already decided by the Egyptian authority, a fact to be dealt with rather than a choice they are making.\n\n\"So send our brother with us so that we may receive our measure\" is the request itself, placed after the problem so that it appears as the only available solution. The family needed food, the supply had been cut off, and one condition would restore it.\n\n\"And we will definitely watch over him.\" is the promise, and it is almost word for word what they said about Joseph decades earlier. On that occasion they had assured their father that they would watch over him, and Jacob had let Joseph go on the strength of it.\n\nThe Quran preserves this echo without comment, and it is devastating. These men either did not notice that they were repeating the exact assurance that preceded the worst act of their lives, or they noticed and said it anyway because they had nothing else to offer.\n\nEither way, the repetition tells the reader something about the state of the family. The old wound had never been dealt with. The lie had never been retracted, the truth had never been spoken, and now the same words were being used again in the same situation.\n\nJacob's reply in the next verse shows that he heard the echo clearly. He had never believed their account of the wolf, and he was not going to pretend otherwise now.\n\nWhat makes this scene painful is that they were, this time, telling the truth. Their need was real, and their intention toward Benjamin was probably sincere. Nobody could tell the difference, and that was their own doing.",
  "64": "\"\"Should I trust you with him as I once trusted you with his brother ˹Joseph˺?\" is Jacob's answer, and after decades of silence on the subject, it is the sharpest thing he says in the chapter.\n\nHe does not accuse them of murder or demand a confession. He simply points out the obvious parallel. They are asking for exactly what they asked for before, with exactly the same promise attached, concerning the only remaining son of the same mother.\n\nThe question does not require an answer. It exposes the position they are in, and it makes clear that Jacob had never been persuaded by the story of the wolf. His earlier response to the bloodied shirt had already indicated as much, when he said that their souls had tempted them to something and that he would endure with beautiful patience. That patience did not mean pretending to believe them.\n\nThere is restraint here that deserves notice. Jacob has been grieving for years, he knows his sons lied, and this is the moment when bitterness could pour out. Instead he asks one question and moves immediately to something else.\n\n\"But ˹only˺ Allah is the best Protector\" is where he turns. Having established that their guarantees are worthless, he does not conclude that Benjamin must never leave the house. He relocates the protection to where it actually resides.\n\nThis is a change in Jacob's own position, and it is significant. When Joseph left, Jacob's expressed fear was about wolves and negligence. Now, older and having lost a son, he entrusts the matter to Allah rather than to anyone's supervision.\n\n\"And He is the Most Merciful of the merciful.\" closes his statement with a plea folded inside a description. An old man who has already lost one beloved child, being asked to release the second, appeals to the quality of Allah that he most needs.\n\nHe agrees, as the following verses show, but on terms and with his heart placed somewhere other than in his sons' promises.",
  "65": "\"When they opened their bags, they discovered that their money had been returned to them\" is the moment Joseph's quiet arrangement takes effect. The discovery happens exactly where he intended, in front of the whole family, at the point when his brothers were struggling to persuade their father.\n\n\"They argued, \"O our father! What more can we ask for?\"\" shows them seizing the moment. The returned money strengthened their case enormously, since it proved the Egyptian official had been generous rather than hostile.\n\nTheir argument then runs through every angle. \"Here is our money, fully returned to us\" establishes the good faith of the man they are dealing with. \"Now we can buy more food for our family\" points to the need that the household actually faces. \"We will watch over our brother\" repeats the promise once more. \"And obtain an extra camel-load of grain\" adds the practical benefit of taking Benjamin, since each person's presence increased the allocation. \"That load can be easily secured\" reassures him that the transaction will be straightforward.\n\nIt is a well-constructed case, and every part of it is true. Their difficulty, as the previous verses showed, is that their word had long ago stopped being worth anything on precisely this subject.\n\nThere is something worth noticing in how they present the matter. Their appeal is entirely practical: money, food, an extra load. Not one word addresses the real obstacle, which is their father's memory of the last son they took away.\n\nIt is also worth observing what the returned money does not do. It does not make them trustworthy. It shows that the Egyptian was generous, which is a fact about him rather than about them.\n\nJacob's answer in the next verse deals with the problem directly. He does not argue with their reasoning, because their reasoning is sound. He asks for something that their promises cannot supply on their own.",
  "66": "\"\"I will not send him with you until you give me a solemn oath by Allah that you will certainly bring him back to me\" is Jacob's condition, and it shows a man who has learned from experience.\n\nLast time he accepted their word. This time he requires an oath sworn by Allah. The Arabic indicates a firm covenant, a binding pledge of the most serious kind. In that world, and in Islamic teaching after it, an oath in Allah's name carries real weight, and breaking one is a grave matter.\n\nWhat Jacob is doing is practical rather than sentimental. He cannot make his sons trustworthy, and he knows it. What he can do is place their commitment under an authority they cannot escape. If their conscience will not hold them, perhaps their fear of Allah will.\n\n\"Unless you are totally overpowered.\" is the exception he allows, and it shows fairness in the middle of his caution. He is not demanding a guarantee against the impossible. If they are overwhelmed by force or circumstances entirely beyond their control, the oath does not bind them.\n\nThis qualification matters. Islamic teaching does not hold people responsible for what genuinely lies outside their capacity, and Jacob's wording reflects that principle. He wants their best effort, not a promise no human being could keep.\n\n\"Then after they had given him their oaths, he concluded, \"Allah is a Witness to what we have said.\"\" seals the agreement. Having received their oaths, he places the whole arrangement under divine observation.\n\nThe phrasing includes everyone. It is not only their promise that Allah witnesses, but the entire exchange, including his own conditions and their acceptance.\n\nThere is a quiet strength in the way Jacob handles this. He does not accuse them again, does not refuse outright, and does not pretend to trust them. He takes the only real precaution available, binds it with an oath, and then hands the matter to the One who actually protects. His instruction in the next verse shows him doing the same thing again, with a practical measure this time.",
  "67": "\"\"O my sons! Do not enter ˹the city˺ all through one gate, but through separate gates.\" is Jacob's final piece of advice before they leave, and commentators have generally explained it as a precaution against attracting attention.\n\nEleven grown brothers arriving together, all sons of one father and evidently a prosperous household, would draw notice in a foreign capital during a famine. Some early commentators mentioned the fear of the evil eye, which is a real concept in Islamic teaching, and others mentioned the risk of arousing suspicion or envy among Egyptian officials. Both concerns fit the situation, and neither contradicts the other.\n\nWhat matters more than the exact reason is the principle Jacob attaches to it, because he immediately guards against a misunderstanding.\n\n\"I cannot help you against ˹what is destined by˺ Allah in the least.\" rules out any idea that his advice is a form of protection in itself. He is a prophet giving sensible instructions, and he states plainly that no arrangement of his can alter what Allah has decreed.\n\nThis is one of the clearest statements in the Quran about the relationship between taking precautions and accepting destiny. Jacob does both at once. He gives practical advice and simultaneously denies that the advice controls anything.\n\n\"It is only Allah Who decides.\" states where authority rests. The Arabic indicates that judgement belongs to Him alone, which is the same phrase Joseph used to the prisoners years earlier in a different context. Father and son speak the same language about the same truth.\n\n\"In Him I put my trust. And in Him let the faithful put their trust.\" completes the instruction by turning it into a general principle for all believers.\n\nThe combination described here is the balanced Islamic position. Tie the camel and rely on Allah, as the Prophet told a man in a hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi. Precautions are taken because we are commanded to act sensibly, and the heart rests elsewhere because outcomes were never in our hands.",
  "68": "\"Then when they entered as their father had instructed them, this did not help them against ˹the Will of˺ Allah whatsoever\" confirms exactly what Jacob had said. They followed his advice, entering through separate gates, and it changed nothing about what was going to happen to them in Egypt.\n\nThe verse states this plainly rather than leaving it implied. Benjamin was going to be detained, the family was going to be tested again, and no arrangement about city gates could prevent it.\n\nThis is not a criticism of the advice. The Quran does not say that Jacob was wrong to give it or that his sons were foolish to follow it. It says the advice could not override a decree, which is precisely what Jacob himself had told them.\n\n\"It was just a desire in Jacob's heart which he satisfied.\" explains the instruction's real function. It was a father's wish to do something for his sons, and acting on it settled something in him.\n\nThere is something deeply human in this observation, and readers who have watched over people they love will recognise it. A parent gives advice partly because it might help and partly because doing nothing is unbearable. The Quran records this without mockery. The need to act on behalf of those we love is treated as a legitimate part of how people are made.\n\n\"He was truly blessed with ˹great˺ knowledge because of what We had taught him\" defends Jacob against any suggestion that his precaution showed weak faith. He knew exactly what he was doing, and his knowledge came from Allah's teaching.\n\nThat knowledge is visible in the balance of his conduct: he took precautions, required an oath, gave practical advice, and stated openly that none of it could resist Allah's decree. Holding all of that together at once is what real understanding looks like.\n\n\"But most people have no knowledge\" contrasts him with the majority, who typically fall into one extreme or the other, either relying entirely on their own arrangements or abandoning effort in the name of trust.",
  "69": "\"When they entered Joseph's presence, he called his brother ˹Benjamin˺ aside\" is the reunion the whole plan was built for. Joseph finds a way to speak with him privately, away from the ten who had separated them decades earlier.\n\nConsider what this meeting meant. Benjamin was a small child when Joseph disappeared, probably too young to have understood what happened. He had grown up as the remaining son of a mother who had died, in a household where his older brothers had already destroyed one boy and lied about it, with a father blind with grief. Whatever his life had been, it had not been easy.\n\n\"And confided ˹to him˺, \"I am indeed your brother ˹Joseph˺!\"\" is the disclosure. Joseph reveals himself to one person and no one else.\n\nThe choice is deliberate and carefully judged. He is not yet ready to confront the ten, and telling Benjamin allowed the boy to understand what was about to happen to him. The plan in the following verses involved Benjamin being publicly accused of theft and detained, and without this conversation he would have been terrified and bewildered.\n\n\"So do not feel distressed about what they have been doing.\" is the reassurance that follows. The Arabic indicates telling him not to grieve or be troubled by their conduct.\n\nCommentators have read this in two ways that both make sense. Joseph may be telling him not to grieve over what the brothers did in the past, since Joseph is alive and the wrong has not destroyed him. Or he may be preparing Benjamin for what the brothers are about to do and say in the immediate future, when the cup is found and they distance themselves from him.\n\nEither way, the verse shows Joseph's care in handling people. He had power, information, and a plan, and his first use of all three was to comfort the person who would be most frightened by what came next.",
  "70": "\"When Joseph had provided them with supplies, he slipped the royal cup into his brother's bag\" describes the step that makes Benjamin's detention possible. The grain is distributed first, as before, and then the cup is placed.\n\nThe plan raises an obvious question, and it deserves a direct answer. Is Joseph deceiving people here? The Quran's own comment, a few verses later, is that this was a plan inspired by Allah, and that Joseph could not otherwise have kept his brother under the king's law. The stratagem was divinely guided, aimed at a good end, and it caused no lasting harm to anyone. Benjamin was in on it, having just been told who Joseph was.\n\nThat last detail matters enormously. Benjamin knew. He was not an innocent victim of a false charge; he was a participant who understood that he was about to be kept in Egypt with his brother.\n\n\"Then a herald cried, \"O people of the caravan! You must be thieves!\"\" is the public accusation, shouted after the caravan had departed. The Arabic is a strong statement, and it would have been humiliating for a group of foreign visitors.\n\nThere is a fine point that commentators have discussed here. The herald's cry is a general accusation against the caravan, and the search that follows is genuine from the searchers' point of view, since the palace staff carrying it out were not necessarily part of the arrangement. Scholars examining the ethics of the episode have noted that the words used are indirect, and that a missing royal cup had in fact been placed among their goods, so the search itself was not built on a fabricated event.\n\nFor the brothers, the moment must have been shocking. They had come as honest buyers, been treated generously twice, and were now being publicly called thieves on the road out of the city.",
  "71": "\"\"What have you lost?\" is the brothers' response as they turn back, and its tone tells us a great deal about their state of mind.\n\nThey do not panic, protest, or flee. They stop, turn around, and ask a practical question. This is the reaction of men who know they have taken nothing. Someone carrying stolen property does not usually invite an inquiry.\n\nThe Arabic indicates them turning toward the herald and his companions, facing the accusation directly rather than avoiding it. Innocence produces a particular kind of composure, and the Quran captures it in a single short line.\n\nThere is also something in the form of their question. They do not begin by denying the charge, which would be the instinct of someone who felt vulnerable. They ask what is missing, which treats the matter as a problem to be solved rather than an attack to be repelled.\n\nFor the reader who knows what has been planted in Benjamin's bag, the scene carries a heavy irony. Their confidence is entirely justified with respect to themselves, and entirely misplaced with respect to what is about to be found.\n\nIt is worth pausing on the position of the brothers here. These were men who had committed a real crime decades earlier and lied about it successfully. Now they stand accused of a crime they did not commit, in a foreign country, with no power to defend themselves beyond their word.\n\nThere is a rough justice in the situation that the Quran does not comment on but allows the reader to feel. They had once brought a false shirt to their father as evidence and asked him to believe a story they had invented. Now they face an accusation they cannot disprove, and the evidence will be found among their own belongings.\n\nTheir confident answer in the next verses, offering their own terms for the punishment of a thief, would make everything much worse for them.",
  "72": "\"\"We have lost the King's measuring cup.\" is the answer, and the object named is significant. It was a royal measure, used in the distribution of grain and belonging to the king himself. Losing such an item in the middle of a famine administration would be a serious matter, not a private inconvenience.\n\nThe description makes the accusation credible. This was not a trinket that might have been mislaid; it was a working instrument of state whose absence would be noticed immediately.\n\n\"And whoever brings it will be awarded a camel-load ˹of grain˺.\" offers a reward, and the choice of reward tells us everything about the times. Not gold, not silver, but food. In a famine, a camel-load of grain was worth more than money, and the brothers themselves had travelled a considerable distance precisely to obtain such loads.\n\nThe offer also serves a practical purpose in the scene. It frames the search as a recovery operation rather than a criminal manhunt, giving the caravan a reason to cooperate rather than resist.\n\n\"I guarantee it.\" is the herald's personal assurance, standing behind the promised reward. The Arabic indicates that he takes responsibility for it, making himself liable for the payment.\n\nThis is an ordinary administrative exchange, and its ordinariness is part of what makes the scene work. Nothing about the herald's words suggests a trap. Officials have lost a valuable item, they are searching the last people who had access to it, and a reward is offered for its return.\n\nThe brothers had no way of reading the situation correctly. Everything visible to them pointed toward a straightforward misunderstanding that would be cleared up as soon as their bags were searched.\n\nTheir reply in the next verse shows how certain they were, and how that certainty led them to say more than they should have. Confidence in one's innocence is a good thing, and it becomes dangerous when it turns into an offer to name one's own penalty.",
  "73": "\"\"By Allah! You know well that we did not come to cause trouble in the land, nor are we thieves.\" is the brothers' defence, and it is delivered with an oath.\n\nThey swear by Allah, which is notable in an Egyptian setting and indicates that these were people known to be believers in the one God. The oath expresses complete confidence, and they were entitled to it: they had not taken the cup.\n\n\"You know well\" is an appeal to the Egyptians' own observation. The brothers had been to Egypt before, dealt honestly, paid for their grain, and returned. Their conduct was on record, and they are asking the officials to weigh it.\n\nTheir two denials cover different ground. The first rejects any intention of causing corruption in the land, which is the accusation a foreign group would most fear in a country under strain. The second rejects theft specifically.\n\nThere is a painful irony in this speech that the Quran leaves for the reader to notice. These men were once thieves of a kind far worse than any cup. They stole a child from his father, sold him into slavery, and lied for decades. The word they use so confidently would have described them precisely in a different context.\n\nThey speak in complete sincerity, and that is what makes it striking. A person can be genuinely innocent of the charge in front of him while carrying something far heavier that nobody has ever accused him of.\n\nThe oath also raises the stakes of what follows. Having sworn by Allah that they are not thieves, they are about to watch a royal cup emerge from one of their own bags in front of witnesses. Their credibility, already destroyed at home, is about to collapse in Egypt as well.\n\nWhat happens next is that they are asked to name the penalty themselves, and their answer will hand Joseph exactly what he needs.",
  "74": "\"\"What should be the price for theft, if you are lying?\" is the question put to the brothers, and it is carefully framed.\n\nThe Egyptians are not asserting guilt. They are asking a procedural question: if the search proves you wrong, what is the appropriate penalty? The Arabic asks about the recompense for theft.\n\nThis is a normal thing to ask a group of foreigners. Legal customs varied between peoples, and the brothers came from a different land with a different tradition. Establishing whose law applies before conducting a search is entirely reasonable, and the question also serves Joseph's purpose precisely, since his plan required the brothers themselves to specify a penalty that Egyptian law did not provide.\n\nThe addition \"if you are lying\" keeps the accusation open without pressing it. Nothing has been proven, and the officials are simply noting the possibility.\n\nThere is something worth observing in how this exchange is structured. The brothers have just sworn a powerful oath of innocence. Asking them, in that moment of confidence, to name the penalty for theft is a question they would answer without hesitation, since in their minds no penalty would ever be applied.\n\nPeople who are certain they cannot lose tend to accept terms they would examine much more carefully if they thought the outcome was in doubt. This happens constantly in ordinary life, in contracts, arguments, and confident promises.\n\nThe Quran does not moralise about this. It simply records the sequence: an oath, a question, and an answer given too quickly.\n\nWhat the brothers say next is entirely honest, reflecting the law of their own people as they understood it, and it will be used exactly as stated. The Quran's comment a few verses later makes clear that this was the point of the whole exchange, since Joseph could not have detained Benjamin under the king's law.",
  "75": "\"\"The price will be ˹the enslavement of˺ the one in whose bag the cup is found.\" is the brothers' answer, and it hands Joseph precisely what his plan required.\n\nUnder the law they knew, a thief could be handed over to the person he had wronged, becoming his property in compensation. Commentators explain that this was the practice among the Children of Israel at the time, and it differed from Egyptian law, which imposed other penalties. By naming their own custom, the brothers made it possible for Benjamin to be kept in Egypt rather than punished under Egyptian rules.\n\n\"That is how we punish the wrongdoers.\" confirms that they are describing an established principle rather than inventing a penalty on the spot. They speak with the confidence of men quoting a familiar law.\n\nIt is worth thinking about why they answered so readily. They were completely certain of their innocence, and a person who knows he cannot lose has no reason to negotiate terms. Their oath in the previous verse and their answer here come from the same place.\n\nThere is also something that they may not have considered. Even if the ten were innocent, they were vouching for a bag they had not packed and could not inspect. Speaking on behalf of others is a serious matter, and they did it without hesitation.\n\nThe Quran presents all of this without dramatic comment, allowing the situation to unfold. What follows is the search, and the sequence of it is described deliberately in the next verse.\n\nFor the reader, there is a heavy sense of a circle closing. These men had once decided the fate of a brother and told their father a story. Now they have pronounced the sentence that will separate them from another brother, in words they chose themselves, in front of the very person they thought was gone.",
  "76": "\"Joseph began searching their bags before that of his brother ˹Benjamin˺, then brought it out of Benjamin's bag\" describes the search with a detail that matters. The other bags were opened first.\n\nThis was necessary for the plan to be credible. Going straight to Benjamin's bag would have exposed the arrangement immediately, since no searcher could know in advance where to look. By starting elsewhere, the search appeared genuine, and the discovery came where nobody expected it.\n\n\"This is how We inspired Joseph to plan.\" is the Quran's own comment on the episode, and it settles the question of whether the stratagem was legitimate. The Arabic indicates that Allah guided this plan for him, and the word used for the plan is the same one used earlier for the schemes worked against him. The difference lies entirely in the purpose.\n\n\"He could not have taken his brother under the King's law\" explains why the plan was necessary. Egyptian law did not permit holding Benjamin, and there was no lawful route by which Joseph could keep him. The brothers' own declaration of their custom created the opening.\n\n\"But Allah had so willed.\" places the whole arrangement where the chapter has consistently placed everything: within a decree that people serve without knowing.\n\n\"We elevate in rank whoever We will.\" speaks to Joseph's own position, raised from a pit to authority, and it is stated as a general principle rather than a one-off event.\n\n\"But above those ranking in knowledge is the One All-Knowing.\" closes the verse with a limit. Joseph knew more than his brothers, more than the Egyptian court, and more than the men searching the bags. Above every level of human knowledge stands the One whose knowledge is complete.\n\nThat final line is a safeguard against the pride that knowledge naturally produces. Whatever a person understands, someone may understand more, and above all of them is Allah.",
  "77": "\"\"If he has stolen, so did his ˹full˺ brother before.\" is the brothers' reaction, and it is one of the ugliest moments in the chapter.\n\nFaced with an accusation against Benjamin, their first instinct is to distance themselves from him. They do not defend him, ask questions, or express doubt. They immediately point out that he comes from a different mother and that his full brother was also a thief.\n\nThe accusation against Joseph is invented. Nothing in the chapter suggests he ever stole anything, and commentators have discussed various stories circulated to explain the remark, none of which is established. What is clear is that they were willing to slander a brother they believed dead in order to protect their own standing in front of an Egyptian official.\n\nNotice also the phrase \"his ˹full˺ brother.\" Decades later, the old division is still there. Joseph and Benjamin remain, in their minds, the sons of the other mother.\n\n\"But Joseph suppressed his outrage—revealing nothing to them\" describes his response, and it is remarkable restraint. He is standing in front of men who are slandering him to his face while he holds complete power over them. The Arabic indicates that he concealed it within himself and did not disclose it.\n\nHe could have ended everything at that moment: revealed his identity, exposed their crime, and had them arrested. He said nothing.\n\n\"And said ˹to himself˺, \"You are in such an evil position, and Allah knows best ˹the truth of˺ what you claim.\"\" is what he kept inside. Even his private judgement is measured. He notes that their situation is worse than they realise, and he leaves the truth of their claim to Allah rather than pronouncing on it himself.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad described real strength in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim as controlling oneself at the moment of anger. Joseph, in a position to do anything he wished, did exactly that.",
  "78": "\"\"O Chief Minister! He has a very old father, so take one of us instead.\" is the brothers' appeal once the cup has been found, and it deserves credit before anything else is said about it.\n\nThey are offering to take Benjamin's place. Whatever their faults, and the chapter has displayed them at length, these are not the same men who threw a boy into a well and walked away. They are trying to protect this brother, even at cost to themselves.\n\nTheir reason is their father. They mention his age, and the appeal is honest: an old man who has already lost one son cannot bear to lose another. Whether their concern is for Jacob's grief, for the oath they swore, or for both, it is a real change from the men who once said that their father was clearly mistaken to love Joseph.\n\nThere is a hard truth in their position, though. The reason this situation is unbearable for Jacob is the crime they committed decades earlier. Had Joseph come home that day, Jacob would have had both sons and this journey would have been an ordinary trading trip.\n\n\"We surely see you as one of the good-doers.\" is how they close, appealing to the character the Egyptian official has shown. He had given full measure, hosted them well, and returned their money.\n\nThe phrase they use is the same description the two prisoners gave Joseph years earlier, and the same word the Quran itself uses for him. Even without knowing who he is, they recognise the quality that has followed him through every stage of his life.\n\nTheir appeal fails, as the next verse shows, and it fails on grounds of justice rather than hardness. What they are asking for is that an innocent man be punished in place of a guilty one, and Joseph will not do it, even though he knows perfectly well that neither of them is guilty of anything.",
  "79": "\"\"Allah forbid that we should take other than the one with whom we found our property.\" is Joseph's refusal, and the principle behind it is one of the foundations of justice in Islam.\n\nPunishment attaches to the person responsible and to nobody else. Taking one of the brothers in Benjamin's place would mean holding an innocent man accountable for someone else's act, and Joseph rejects the idea with an expression that seeks refuge in Allah from even considering it.\n\nThe Quran states this principle repeatedly in other places, most directly in the statement that no bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another. It appears in several chapters, and it rules out the punishment of families, tribes, or communities for the acts of individuals. In a world where collective punishment was normal, and where a man's relatives could be seized for his crime, this was a significant standard.\n\n\"Otherwise, we would surely be unjust.\" explains the consequence of departing from it. The Arabic word indicates wrongdoing, putting something where it does not belong, and punishing the wrong person is exactly that.\n\nThere is a layer of irony here that the chapter leaves unspoken. Joseph knows that Benjamin took nothing, and he is refusing to release him on grounds of justice. The refusal is stated in terms of the evidence as it stands publicly, and Joseph does not lie: he says they may take only the one with whom the property was found, which is factually accurate.\n\nWhat makes the moment work is that his brothers cannot argue with the principle. They had named the penalty themselves, and they had sworn they were honest men. Now they are being held to a standard of exact justice that they cannot fault.\n\nFor the reader, there is something instructive in watching a man exercise power while refusing the shortcuts that power offers. Joseph could have accepted the swap and pleased everyone; he declined because the outcome would have been unjust in form.",
  "80": "\"When they lost all hope in him, they spoke privately\" marks the point where the brothers accept that the Egyptian official will not change his mind. The Arabic indicates complete despair of persuading him, and they withdraw to confer among themselves.\n\n\"The eldest of them said, \"Do you not know that your father had taken a solemn oath by Allah from you\"\" reminds them of the pledge they gave before leaving home. This is the same brother, most likely, who had once argued against killing Joseph and proposed the well instead. He appears again at the decisive moment as the voice of restraint.\n\nThe oath is the immediate problem. They swore by Allah to bring Benjamin back, and they cannot.\n\n\"Nor how you failed him regarding Joseph before?\" is the second half of his reminder, and it is the first time in the chapter that any of them refers openly to what they did. Decades of silence, and now, with everything collapsing, the old crime is named among themselves.\n\nNotice how he phrases it: how you failed him. He includes himself in the group only partially, since he had opposed the worst of it, but he does not pretend the family's history is irrelevant. What is happening now cannot be separated from what happened then.\n\n\"So I am not leaving this land until my father allows me to, or Allah decides for me.\" is his decision. He will remain in Egypt rather than return and face Jacob with a second missing son. This is partly shame and partly a refusal to break the oath by simply walking away.\n\n\"For He is the Best of Judges.\" closes his statement by placing the outcome with Allah, which is exactly what his father had done when he handed Benjamin over.\n\nThere is something in this whole scene worth noticing. The brothers are trapped by promises they cannot keep and a history they cannot escape, and they are beginning, finally, to see the connection between the two.",
  "81": "\"\"Return to your father and say, 'O our father! Your son committed theft.\"\" is the message the eldest brother instructs the others to deliver, and every word of it was carefully chosen.\n\nNotice that they are told to say \"your son\" rather than \"our brother.\" Commentators have observed this, and it reflects the distance they had always kept from Benjamin. Even in a moment of genuine concern for him, the old separation shows.\n\nThe report itself is what they were required to give. From their position, it was simply the truth as they had witnessed it: the cup was found in his bag, and the penalty followed under the terms they themselves had named.\n\n\"We testify only to what we know.\" is the crucial qualification, and it shows a caution they had entirely lacked decades earlier. They are limiting their statement to what they actually saw, rather than claiming knowledge of what really happened.\n\nThe contrast with their earlier conduct is unmistakable. When Joseph disappeared, they invented a wolf, produced a false shirt, and presented a story as established fact. Now they refuse to go beyond the evidence.\n\nWhether they had genuinely learned or were simply being careful, the change is real. The Quran records it without praising or condemning, letting the reader compare the two scenes.\n\n\"We could not guard against the unforeseen.\" is their defence regarding the oath they swore. Jacob had required them to bring Benjamin back unless they were totally overpowered, and they are invoking that exception. What happened was outside their control and beyond their ability to prevent.\n\nTheir argument is reasonable. They could not have known what was in the bag, could not have prevented the search, and could not have overturned the ruling of an Egyptian minister.\n\nThere is still something unsaid in their message. They are describing an unforeseeable event, and the reason their father will struggle to accept it is a history they created themselves. That connection, which the eldest brother had just named among themselves, does not appear in the message they send home.",
  "82": "\"\"Ask ˹the people of˺ the land where we were and the caravan we travelled with.\" is the brothers' invitation to verify their account, and it represents a striking change from the last time they brought bad news home.\n\nThey are pointing to independent witnesses. The people of the Egyptian city where the incident occurred saw the search and the ruling. The other travellers in the caravan, unrelated to their family, witnessed the whole episode on the road. Any of them could confirm what happened.\n\nCompare this with the shirt covered in false blood. On that occasion the only evidence was something they had manufactured themselves, and the only witnesses were the ones telling the story. Here they invite outside confirmation from people who have no reason to protect them.\n\n\"We are certainly telling the truth.\" is the claim they attach to it, and this time it is accurate.\n\nThere is a bitter difficulty in their position, though, and it is entirely of their own making. A person who has lied about exactly this kind of event cannot simply announce that he is telling the truth now and expect to be believed. Trust, once destroyed, does not return because the liar has become honest.\n\nThis is one of the most practical lessons in the chapter. The cost of a serious lie is not only the harm it causes at the time. It is the permanent damage to a person's credibility, which then affects every honest thing he says afterwards, sometimes for the rest of his life.\n\nTheir father's reaction in the next verse confirms exactly this. He does not investigate, does not send anyone to ask the caravan, and does not weigh their evidence. He responds with the same words he used decades earlier when they brought him the shirt, because in his mind the two events belong together.\n\nWhether he was right to doubt them this time is a separate question. What the chapter shows is that they had left him no way to tell the difference.",
  "83": "\"\"No! Your souls must have tempted you to do something ˹evil˺.\" is Jacob's response, and it is word for word what he said when they brought him Joseph's bloodied shirt.\n\nThe repetition is deliberate and devastating. Decades have passed, and he answers the second loss exactly as he answered the first. In his mind, the same men have done the same kind of thing again.\n\nWhether he was correct is a fair question, and this time they were largely innocent of what he suspected. The Quran does not comment on his accuracy, and there is no need to defend him as infallible in a judgement about his sons' motives. What it records is the reaction of a father who has been deceived once on precisely this matter.\n\n\"So ˹I am left with nothing but˺ beautiful patience!\" is also a repetition of his earlier response. The Arabic indicates patience without complaint to people, without bitterness, without collapse.\n\nThere is something extraordinary in a man reaching for the same discipline a second time. Patience the first time might be attributed to shock. Patience after a second identical blow, in old age, having already lost his sight to grief, is a settled character rather than a reaction.\n\n\"I trust Allah will return them all to me.\" is new, and it is the most remarkable line in the verse. He says all of them, using a plural that must include Joseph.\n\nAfter all these years, Jacob has not given up on his first son. Nothing in the visible world supported this hope. His sons had reported a wolf, the family had mourned, and decades had passed in silence. His expectation rested entirely on his trust in Allah.\n\n\"Surely He ˹alone˺ is the All-Knowing, All-Wise.\" explains where that hope comes from. Jacob does not claim to know what happened or how it will be resolved. He affirms that Allah knows what he does not, and arranges matters with a wisdom he cannot see.",
  "84": "\"He turned away from them, lamenting, \"Alas, poor Joseph!\"\" shows what was actually happening inside Jacob. His sons have just told him about Benjamin, and the cry that escapes him is for Joseph.\n\nThe old grief had never been resolved. A new loss simply opened it, which is exactly how buried sorrow behaves. Anyone who has watched a fresh bereavement pull an older one to the surface will recognise this precisely.\n\nHe turns away from them, which suggests both a physical movement and a withdrawal. He does not shout at his sons or interrogate them further. He removes himself and grieves.\n\n\"And his eyes turned white out of the grief he suppressed.\" describes the physical cost. Commentators explain this as the loss of his sight, brought on by prolonged weeping and sorrow.\n\nThe phrase about suppression is the key to understanding him. The Arabic indicates that he was filled with grief that he held back, containing it rather than releasing it against anyone. This is what his beautiful patience actually consisted of: not the absence of pain, but the refusal to let pain spill out as complaint against Allah or accusation against people.\n\nIt is important to say clearly that his weeping was not a failure of patience. He wept for years and lost his sight, and the Quran presents him throughout as a prophet of exemplary conduct. Islamic teaching distinguishes between grief expressed naturally and grief that turns into protest against the decree. The Prophet Muhammad wept when his own son died and said, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, that the eye sheds tears and the heart grieves, but we say only what pleases our Lord.\n\nThere is a further point worth noticing. Jacob was a prophet, and he did not know that his son was alive and governing Egypt. Prophets are given knowledge of the unseen only when Allah discloses it, and this was withheld from him for decades. His hope, expressed in the previous verse, was faith rather than information.",
  "85": "\"\"By Allah! You will not cease to remember Joseph until you lose your health or ˹even˺ your life.\" is what his sons say to Jacob as they watch him grieve, and it is worth reading their words carefully before judging them.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that he will keep remembering Joseph until he becomes worn out and wasted away, or until he is among those who perish. They are describing a man destroying himself with sorrow.\n\nTheir tone is difficult to place with certainty. It may be exasperation, since decades of mourning had presumably shaped the whole household. It may be genuine alarm at his physical decline. It may include guilt, since they knew what they had done and had to watch its effects every day.\n\nMost likely it contains all three, which is how families usually speak in situations of this kind.\n\nWhat is unmistakable is that they still cannot say the one thing that would help. The truth, that they had sold him and lied, remained locked away, and instead they complain about the symptom while concealing the cause.\n\nThere is something instructive in this for anyone who has tried to comfort a grieving person. Telling someone that their sorrow is damaging them almost never reduces it. Jacob knew perfectly well what his grief was costing him; his eyes had already failed. Being informed of the obvious added nothing.\n\nThe Quran also lets the reader see how sustained mourning appears to those watching from outside. To the sons, it looked like an old man refusing to move on from a loss that everyone else had accepted long ago. From within, as the next verse shows, it was something entirely different.\n\nJacob's reply corrects them without any anger. He does not defend the intensity of his grief, and he does not argue about whether it is reasonable. He simply tells them where he takes it, and hints that he knows something they do not.",
  "86": "\"\"I complain of my anguish and sorrow only to Allah.\" is Jacob's reply, and it is one of the most important sentences in the chapter for understanding what patience actually means.\n\nHe is not denying his pain. He names it directly using two words. The Arabic \"bath\" refers to a grief so severe that a person cannot contain it, sorrow that spills out. The second word indicates deep, quiet sadness. Together they describe a man in considerable distress.\n\nWhat he specifies is the direction of the complaint. He takes it to Allah, and only to Allah. He does not pour it out on his sons, does not resent his situation, and does not question the decree.\n\nThis settles a common misunderstanding about the beautiful patience he claimed earlier. Some imagine that patience requires silence about suffering, so that any expression of pain is a failure. Jacob's example shows otherwise. He wept until he lost his sight, and he described his own condition in strong terms. What he did not do was complain about Allah to people.\n\nIslamic teaching preserves this distinction carefully. Complaining to Allah is worship, and the Quran is full of prophets doing exactly that. Job called on his Lord describing the harm that had touched him. Moses spoke of his need. Zechariah described his weakness and his grey hair. None of this diminished them.\n\n\"And I know from Allah what you do not know.\" is the second half of his reply, and it is where his hope comes from. He is claiming knowledge that his sons lack, and the next verse shows what he does with it: he sends them to search for Joseph.\n\nCommentators have discussed what this knowledge was. Many understood it as his awareness of Allah's mercy and his confidence in the dream Joseph had described as a boy, which had never been fulfilled. Something in him knew the story was not finished.",
  "87": "\"\"O my sons! Go and search ˹diligently˺ for Joseph and his brother.\" is an instruction that must have astonished them. After decades of mourning, their father sends them to look for a man everyone else considered long dead.\n\nHe mentions Joseph first, which tells us where his mind had been all along. The immediate crisis concerned Benjamin, and Jacob's instruction begins with the son who disappeared a lifetime ago.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a careful, thorough search, seeking out news and investigating. This is not a vague hope but a command to act on it.\n\nConsider what this required of the brothers. They knew Joseph's fate, or thought they did, since they had left him in a well and later heard nothing. Being told to search for him meant returning to a subject they had spent decades avoiding.\n\n\"And do not lose hope in the mercy of Allah\" is the reason he gives, and it is the heart of the verse. The Arabic word indicates the particular kind of despair that comes from concluding that relief will never arrive.\n\nJacob is an old blind man who has lost two sons and has no evidence whatsoever that either is alive. He is the person in the story with the most reason to give up, and he is the one forbidding despair.\n\n\"For no one loses hope in Allah's mercy except those with no faith.\" explains why despair is so serious. It is not merely a mood or a weakness of temperament. Concluding that relief is impossible amounts to a statement about Allah, suggesting that His mercy has limits or that His power has run out.\n\nThis is why the Quran treats despair as a matter of belief rather than emotion. Surah az-Zumar carries the same message in its most famous form, telling those who have wronged themselves greatly not to despair of the mercy of Allah, since He forgives all sins.\n\nJacob's hope, as the chapter is about to show, turned out to be entirely justified.",
  "88": "\"\"O Chief Minister! We and our family have been touched with hardship\" is how the brothers open their third appearance before Joseph, and their condition has changed completely.\n\nThe first time they came as buyers with money. The second time they came under pressure but still able to pay. Now they arrive describing hardship that has reached the whole household.\n\n\"And we have brought only a few worthless coins\" admits their poverty openly. The Arabic indicates goods of little value, merchandise that barely counts. They are effectively saying that what they have to offer is not enough for what they need.\n\n\"But ˹please˺ give us our supplies in full and be charitable to us.\" is the request, and its two halves are different in kind. They ask for full measure, which is a matter of fair dealing, and then they ask for charity, which is asking for something they cannot pay for.\n\nThis is the position of men who have run out of options. Proud men from an established family are standing in front of a foreign official asking for a handout.\n\n\"Indeed, Allah rewards the charitable.\" is how they close, appealing to the reward that Allah gives to those who give.\n\nThere is something remarkable in the arrangement here. The men who once complained that their father's affection was misdirected, who plotted against a child out of jealousy over favour, are now dependent on the generosity of that same child.\n\nNotice too that they invoke Allah's reward for charity while speaking to a man they believe to be an Egyptian minister. It suggests they were appealing to a shared moral principle rather than to Egyptian religion, and Joseph would have heard the irony in it.\n\nThe scene is set for the moment the chapter has been building toward since the well. They are at their lowest, entirely at his mercy, and asking for kindness. What he says next changes everything.",
  "89": "\"\"Do you remember what you did to Joseph and his brother in your ignorance?\" is the question that ends Joseph's concealment, and the way he asks it is extraordinary.\n\nHe does not announce his identity. He does not accuse them. He asks whether they remember, which invites them to confront their own act rather than being told about it.\n\nThe mention of both Joseph and his brother is significant. They had wronged Joseph directly, and they had wronged Benjamin too, by leaving him to grow up in a family that treated him as an outsider, and by their readiness to slander him when the cup was found.\n\n\"In your ignorance\" is the phrase that reveals the most about the speaker. Joseph gives them a description of their state at the time rather than a label for their character. The Arabic indicates acting without knowledge or restraint, behaving foolishly.\n\nThis is the same word Joseph used about himself years earlier, when he prayed that he might yield to temptation and fall into ignorance. He understood the category from the inside, as something a person can fall into rather than something only monsters do.\n\nIt is a generous framing, and it is offered before they have said a word in their own defence. He is giving them a way to acknowledge the act without being crushed by it.\n\nConsider the restraint this required. He had waited decades for this moment, had watched them slander him to his face two verses earlier, and now held every advantage. What he produced was a question, phrased with mercy already built into it.\n\nTheir reaction in the next verse shows that the question did what it was meant to do. Something in the way he spoke, perhaps his knowledge of details no Egyptian could have, perhaps something in his face or voice at last recognised, made them see who was standing in front of them.",
  "90": "\"\"Are you really Joseph?\" is the brothers' reaction, and the Quran captures their shock in four words. Something in his question had made the impossible suddenly obvious.\n\nCommentators have suggested that his mention of details only Joseph could know, or perhaps a gesture or a look, finally opened their eyes. Whatever it was, the recognition arrived all at once after years of standing in front of him without seeing.\n\n\"He said, \"I am Joseph, and here is my brother ˹Benjamin˺!\"\" confirms it plainly. He does not draw out the moment or make them wait. He identifies himself and immediately places Benjamin beside him, showing that the brother they thought imprisoned as a thief is safe and with him.\n\n\"Allah has truly been gracious to us.\" is his first comment on the whole situation, and its direction is worth noticing. He does not begin with what they did to him. He begins with what Allah did for both of them.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a favour bestowed. Joseph is describing his rescue from the well, his rise in Egypt, and his reunion with Benjamin as gifts rather than as achievements.\n\n\"Surely whoever is mindful ˹of Allah˺ and patient, then certainly Allah never discounts the reward of the good-doers.\" is the principle he draws from his own life, and it is a summary of the entire chapter.\n\nTwo qualities are named. Mindfulness of Allah, which is the \"taqwa\" that kept him from betraying his master's house in a locked room. And patience, which carried him through the pit, slavery, false accusation, and years in prison.\n\nThe promise attached to them is the one stated earlier in this chapter: nothing done well is ever lost. Joseph is now standing in a position where he can see that his own record was intact all along, even during the decades when nothing about his life suggested it.\n\nHe states this in front of the men who caused most of it, and he states it as encouragement rather than accusation.",
  "91": "\"\"By Allah! Allah has truly preferred you over us, and we have surely been sinful.\" is the brothers' response, and it is a complete confession.\n\nThey swear by Allah, as they had done earlier when denying theft, but this time the oath supports the truth rather than concealing it.\n\n\"Allah has truly preferred you over us\" is the acknowledgement that ends the argument they began the chapter with. Decades earlier they had complained that their father favoured Joseph and declared that Jacob was clearly mistaken. Now they admit that Allah Himself had chosen Joseph, and that their father's instinct had been sound all along.\n\nThis is a hard thing to say. They are conceding that the resentment which drove their entire crime was built on a false judgement, and that the brother they tried to remove was in fact the one Allah had selected.\n\n\"And we have surely been sinful.\" is the admission of guilt, without excuse or explanation. They do not blame their youth, their jealousy, or Satan. They do not point out that one of them argued against killing him. They state what they were.\n\nThere is something valuable in the plainness of it. Genuine repentance in Islamic teaching involves acknowledging the wrong without minimising it, and the Quran preserves their words as an example of exactly that.\n\nConsider what made this possible. Joseph had asked his question in a way that left room for them to answer honestly. He had not humiliated them, had not listed their crimes, and had not demanded anything. Given space, they used it to confess rather than to defend themselves.\n\nThat sequence is worth reflecting on. People pressed into a corner usually justify themselves; people offered a way to be honest sometimes take it.\n\nWhat they do not do is ask for forgiveness. They admit the wrong and stop there, perhaps because they could not imagine it being granted. Joseph's answer in the next verse gives it before they can ask.",
  "92": "\"\"There is no blame on you today.\" is Joseph's response to their confession, and it may be the most generous sentence spoken by any human being in the Quran.\n\nThey had thrown him into a well as a child, sold him into slavery, lied to their father for decades, and slandered him in front of an Egyptian court a few days earlier. He now holds power over their lives and their food supply, and they have just admitted everything.\n\nHe releases them completely. The Arabic indicates that no reproach or reproof will be directed at them. There will be no punishment, no conditions, and not even a lecture.\n\nThe word \"today\" is worth noticing. Some commentators have read it as emphasising the finality of the pardon, closing the matter permanently from this day onward.\n\n\"May Allah forgive you!\" goes further still. Having pardoned his own claim, he prays for them to be forgiven by Allah, which is the only forgiveness that can settle the part of their sin that was not against him.\n\nThis distinction matters in Islamic teaching. A wrong against a person requires that person's pardon; a wrong against Allah requires His. Joseph gives what is his to give and asks for the rest on their behalf.\n\n\"He is the Most Merciful of the merciful!\" is the same description Jacob used when he entrusted Benjamin to Allah's protection. Father and son reach for the same words.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad echoed this scene at the conquest of Mecca. Standing before the people who had persecuted him, driven him out, and fought him for twenty years, he asked what they expected of him, and when they appealed to his brotherhood, he told them to go, for they were free. Reports of this occasion mention that he quoted Joseph's words: no blame upon you today.\n\nForgiveness of this kind is only possible for someone who has genuinely handed the matter to Allah.",
  "93": "\"\"Go with this shirt of mine and cast it over my father's face, and he will regain his sight.\" is Joseph's instruction after forgiving them, and it moves immediately from pardon to repair.\n\nThere is a striking symmetry in the object he chooses. A shirt had begun the family's tragedy, when they brought Jacob a garment stained with false blood. A shirt had marked Joseph's vindication in Egypt, when the tear at the back proved his innocence. Now a shirt is sent to undo the damage the first one caused.\n\nThe Quran does not explain how a garment restores sight, and there is no need to speculate. It was a miracle granted at Allah's command, using an item connected to the son whose loss had caused the blindness in the first place.\n\nNotice that Joseph does not send a message explaining himself, nor does he travel to his father immediately. He sends the shirt with the brothers, which requires them to be the ones who deliver the good news. The men who brought Jacob the false shirt must now carry the true one.\n\nThis is a quiet act of restoration for them as well as for their father. They had broken the family; they are given a share in mending it.\n\n\"Then come back to me with your whole family.\" is the second instruction, and it settles the future. Egypt had grain, Joseph had authority, and the famine still had years to run. The entire household would relocate.\n\nThis is the beginning of the Children of Israel's presence in Egypt, which lasted for centuries and eventually led to the enslavement that Moses was sent to end. The Quran does not draw that line here, but a reader who knows the later story sees the arc: a family arriving as honoured guests of a minister, and their descendants leaving as fugitives crossing a sea.\n\nFor the moment, the instruction is simply a reunion, arranged by the son they abandoned.",
  "94": "\"When the caravan departed ˹from Egypt˺, their father said ˹to those around him˺, \"You may think I am senile, but I certainly sense the smell of Joseph.\"\" describes something that happened hundreds of miles away from Egypt at the moment the caravan set out.\n\nJacob perceives his son. The Arabic indicates finding or sensing the scent, and commentators have understood it as a genuine perception granted to him by Allah rather than an ordinary use of the nose. Distance and wind cannot explain it, and the verse does not offer a natural explanation.\n\nThe way he introduces it is deeply human. He anticipates the reaction before it comes, saying that they will think he is losing his mind. He knows exactly how it will sound.\n\nThat anticipation tells us something about his life in those years. He had spent decades being treated as an old man who could not let go, whose grief had become an embarrassment to those around him. The previous verses recorded his sons saying almost that to his face.\n\nThere is a striking irony in the situation. Jacob, blind and mocked as senile, perceives the truth. His sons, with working eyes and full knowledge of what had actually happened, had spent three journeys standing in front of Joseph without recognising him.\n\nThe Quran does not comment on this, and it does not need to. The chapter has shown throughout that seeing is not a matter of eyesight.\n\nThere is also something worth noticing about the timing. This perception comes to him at the very moment the caravan leaves Egypt, before any message could possibly have arrived, and before he has any reason to expect news. Jacob had been telling his sons for some time that he knew from Allah what they did not know, and this is that knowledge showing itself.\n\nTheir response, recorded in the next verse, is exactly what he predicted.",
  "95": "\"\"By Allah! You are definitely still in your old delusion.\" is the reply Jacob receives from those around him, and it is as dismissive as he had expected.\n\nThe Arabic word they use indicates being astray or mistaken, and the same root appeared at the very start of the chapter when the brothers said their father was clearly mistaken to favour Joseph. Decades have passed, and the household is still describing his attachment to Joseph in the same terms.\n\nNotice the word \"old.\" They are saying this is nothing new, just the same error he has been making for years. Their patience with him had clearly worn thin, and they treat his statement as another symptom rather than as information.\n\nCommentators note that these were probably members of the household present with him rather than the brothers travelling with the shirt, since those were still on the road. Either way, the response represents the settled opinion of the people around him.\n\nThere is a hard truth in this scene about how people treat those who hold on to hope longer than others think reasonable. A person who keeps expecting relief after everyone else has moved on becomes an object of pity, and eventually of irritation. Jacob was told, in effect, that his faith was a delusion.\n\nWhat makes the moment sharper is that they swear by Allah while saying it. They are entirely certain, and they are entirely wrong. Confidence is not evidence, and a majority verdict about someone's mental state is worth nothing when the minority happens to be right.\n\nThe Quran resolves this quickly. The next verse describes the arrival of the messenger with the shirt, and Jacob's sight returning. Within a very short space, the man dismissed as deluded is proved to have been perceiving something real all along.\n\nHis response when that happens is measured, and it contains no triumph over the people who had just insulted him.",
  "96": "\"But when the bearer of the good news arrived, he cast the shirt over Jacob's face, so he regained his sight.\" is the fulfilment of Joseph's instruction, and the Quran reports it without drama.\n\nThe messenger is described by his function: the one bringing good news. Commentators have suggested various identities, and the chapter does not name him. What matters is that the shirt arrived and the sight returned exactly as Joseph had said it would.\n\nThe healing here is a miracle, granted by Allah's permission. Nothing in the fabric had healing properties, and Joseph himself made no claim of that kind. It was a sign attached to a particular moment in a particular family's story, and it should not be turned into a general principle about garments or relics.\n\n\"Jacob then said ˹to his children˺, \"Did I not tell you that I truly know from Allah what you do not know?\"\" is his response, and it is worth reading carefully for what it does not contain.\n\nHe does not rebuke them for calling him deluded a few verses earlier. He does not say that he was right and they were wrong about his mental state. He does not mention the wolf, the shirt, or the decades of deception.\n\nWhat he does is point back to something he had told them repeatedly: that his knowledge came from Allah. His vindication is used to establish that point rather than to score against them.\n\nThe statement also settles a question the reader may have carried through the chapter. Jacob's hope was not stubbornness or the refusal of an old man to accept reality. It was based on something real that had been given to him, though he could not prove it to anyone.\n\nThere is comfort in this for believers whose confidence in Allah's promise is treated as naivety by people around them. Jacob spent decades being wrong in everyone's eyes, and the verdict was reversed in a single afternoon.",
  "97": "\"\"O our father! Pray for the forgiveness of our sins. We have certainly been sinful.\" is the brothers' request once the truth is fully in the open, and it completes their repentance.\n\nThey had already confessed to Joseph in Egypt, admitting they had been sinful. Now they confess to their father, who was the other person they had wronged. The deception about the wolf, the false shirt, the decades of grief they caused him: all of it was against Jacob directly.\n\nThis is an important feature of genuine repentance in Islamic teaching. Wrongs done to people require that those people be approached. It is not enough to feel sorry privately or to seek Allah's forgiveness alone while the person harmed remains deceived.\n\nTheir request is for his prayer. They ask him to seek forgiveness for them, which is something the Quran permits and encourages: asking a righteous person to pray on one's behalf. It is not asking Jacob to forgive their sins, which is not in his power, but asking him to intercede in supplication.\n\n\"We have certainly been sinful.\" repeats what they said to Joseph, using the same admission. There is no attempt to soften it now that they are home, and no version of the story in which they were partly justified.\n\nNotice what has finally changed in this family. For decades the central fact of their life was a secret that nobody would name. Now it has been spoken aloud twice, once in Egypt and once here, and the concealment is over.\n\nThe Quran does not record Jacob's reaction to learning what really happened, and the omission is striking. There is no scene of accusation or grief over the discovery. The very next verse is his answer, and it contains no reproach at all.\n\nWhatever he felt on finally hearing the truth from his sons' own mouths, what the chapter preserves is his response, which is entirely about forgiveness and mercy.",
  "98": "\"\"I will pray to my Lord for your forgiveness.\" is Jacob's answer, and its simplicity is remarkable given what he has just learned.\n\nHe now knows that his sons took Joseph, disposed of him, lied about the wolf, produced a false shirt, and allowed him to grieve for decades while they knew the truth every day. His response is to agree to pray for them.\n\nThere is no accusation, no demand for an explanation, and no expression of anger. The Quran records none of it, and its silence is louder than any speech would be.\n\nCommentators have noticed that he says he will pray, using a form that indicates future action rather than an immediate supplication. Some early scholars suggested he intended to wait for a time when prayers are most likely to be answered, and several reports mention the hours before dawn. This is a suggestion drawn from the wording rather than something the Quran states, and it should be held lightly.\n\nWhat is clear is that he agrees. He does not withhold his prayer as leverage, does not make them earn it, and does not attach conditions.\n\n\"He ˹alone˺ is indeed the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" explains where his confidence comes from. He is not promising that his prayer will guarantee anything. He is pointing to the qualities of the One being asked.\n\nThe two names are the ones the Quran attaches to repentance throughout. Forgiveness covers what has been done; mercy provides what comes after.\n\nThere is a pattern worth noticing across this whole family. Joseph forgave and prayed that Allah would forgive them. Jacob now does the same. Both had suffered enormously at the hands of these men, and both responded by asking for their pardon rather than their punishment.\n\nThat consistency is not accidental. It is the product of a household shaped by prophethood, in which the response to being wronged had been learned rather than invented in the moment.",
  "99": "\"When they entered Joseph's presence, he received his parents ˹graciously˺\" describes the reunion the whole chapter has been moving toward. The family travelled from Canaan to Egypt, and Joseph met them.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that he drew his parents close to himself, taking them into his embrace and his shelter. The word suggests both physical closeness and the act of taking someone under one's protection.\n\nThe mention of parents raises a question commentators have discussed. Jacob's wife Rachel, Joseph's mother, is generally understood to have died earlier, and many explained the reference as being to his father and his aunt, who had married Jacob and raised him, since Arabic usage allows a stepmother or aunt in that role to be called a mother. Others held that the plural simply refers to Jacob and his wife present at the time. The Quran does not settle the detail, and nothing important depends on it.\n\nWhat matters is the manner of the meeting. Joseph was the second most powerful man in Egypt, receiving elderly relatives who arrived from a famine-struck land as displaced people. He honoured them.\n\n\"And said, \"Enter Egypt, Allah willing, in security.\"\" is his welcome, and every word of it carries weight.\n\nHe invites them into the country as guests rather than as refugees dependent on charity. He offers security, which was the one thing this family had lacked for decades: safety from hunger, from separation, and from the fear that had shadowed them since Joseph disappeared.\n\nThe phrase \"Allah willing\" is worth noticing on the lips of a man who controlled Egypt's resources. He could guarantee their food, their housing, and their legal protection, and he still attached the condition that runs through all Muslim speech about the future. Authority had not made him forget where outcomes come from.\n\nThe Quran instructs believers elsewhere never to say they will do something tomorrow without adding this qualification, and here a man with real power demonstrates it naturally.",
  "100": "\"Then he raised his parents to the throne, and they all fell down in prostration to Joseph\" is the fulfilment of a dream described in the fourth verse of this chapter. Eleven stars, the sun, and the moon had bowed before a boy, and now his parents and eleven brothers bow before a minister of Egypt.\n\nThe prostration requires explanation, since prostration to anyone other than Allah is forbidden in Islam. Commentators explain that this was a gesture of honour and greeting permitted in earlier communities, not an act of worship. The Prophet Muhammad closed that door completely, saying, in a hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi and others, that if he were to order anyone to prostrate to another he would order a wife to prostrate to her husband, and then refusing to permit it. What was allowed as a mark of respect in earlier law is no longer allowed at all.\n\n\"O my dear father! This is the interpretation of my old dream. My Lord has made it come true.\" is Joseph's first comment, addressed to Jacob with the same affectionate term he used as a boy.\n\nHe does not claim the fulfilment as his own achievement. He says his Lord made it real.\n\n\"He was truly kind to me when He freed me from prison, and brought you all from the desert\" lists the favours, and something in the list is striking. He mentions the prison and the reunion, and he says nothing about the well. Commentators have noted this repeatedly: he avoids naming the crime that would embarrass his brothers, even at the moment of his own vindication.\n\n\"After Satan had ignited rivalry between me and my siblings\" is as close as he comes to it, and even here he assigns the blame to Satan rather than to them.\n\n\"Indeed my Lord is subtle in fulfilling what He wills.\" names the quality that explains the entire chapter. The Arabic \"latif\" indicates One whose arrangement is gentle, precise, and works through means too fine to notice.",
  "101": "\"\"My Lord! You have surely granted me authority and taught me the interpretation of dreams.\" begins Joseph's prayer at the summit of his life, and it starts by naming the two gifts he had received.\n\nAuthority over Egypt and knowledge of dreams were the outward marks of his success, and he lists them as things granted rather than things earned. He had asked for the storehouses himself, and he still describes the position as given.\n\n\"˹O˺ Originator of the heavens and the earth!\" is how he addresses Allah, and the choice of description is significant. Standing in the greatest empire of his age, surrounded by monuments to human power, he turns to the One who brought the heavens and earth into being from nothing.\n\n\"You are my Guardian in this world and the Hereafter.\" names the relationship that mattered to him. The Arabic \"wali\" indicates a protector, ally, and close supporter. Joseph had been without any human protector for most of his life: no family in Egypt, no tribe, no advocate. He is saying that the protection he actually had was never in doubt.\n\n\"Allow me to die as one who submits and join me with the righteous.\" is his request, and it is the most surprising part of the prayer.\n\nHe asks for nothing worldly. Not a longer reign, not continued success, not security for his position or his family's new life in Egypt. He asks to die as a Muslim, one who surrenders to Allah, and to be gathered with the righteous.\n\nThis is the prayer of a man who has just received everything the world can offer and has correctly assessed its value. He knew, as the chapter stated earlier, that the reward of the Hereafter is better.\n\nCommentators have observed that this is the first recorded request for death in Islamic tradition, and they clarify that he is not asking to die now, but asking that whenever death comes, it finds him in a state of submission. That distinction matters, since the Prophet Muhammad forbade wishing for death because of hardship.",
  "102": "\"That is from the stories of the unseen which We reveal to you ˹O Prophet˺.\" steps outside the narrative now that it has ended, and speaks about the narrative itself. The story is complete, and the Quran returns to the point it made before beginning.\n\nThe Arabic term for the unseen covers what lies beyond human perception, including a past that nobody alive witnessed and no reliable record preserved.\n\n\"You were not present when they ˹all˺ made up their minds, and when they plotted ˹against Joseph˺.\" specifies the claim with precision. The Prophet was not there when the brothers reached their decision and worked out their scheme.\n\nThis is a striking choice of moment. Of everything in the chapter, the Quran singles out the private conversation among the brothers, the discussion nobody outside that group ever heard. Even Jacob never learned exactly what was said. Yet the chapter reported it in detail: their complaint about their father's favour, the proposal to kill, the alternative of the well, the plan to repent afterwards.\n\nThe argument is straightforward. Knowledge of a private conversation from centuries earlier, in another land, could only come from one source. The Prophet had no access to Jewish scriptures, could not read, and lived among people equally unfamiliar with these accounts in detail. The version preserved in the older texts differs from this one in significant ways, which makes the suggestion of borrowing difficult to sustain.\n\nThe verse also has an effect on how the whole chapter should be received. This has been a long, absorbing story with characters, dialogue, and a satisfying resolution. It would be easy to enjoy it as a story and move on. The reminder that it is revelation about real events, delivered as evidence, prevents that.\n\nFor the Meccan audience the point was immediate. A man they had known all his life was reciting the private deliberations of ancient people, and they had no explanation for where it came from.",
  "103": "\"And most people will not believe—no matter how keen you are\" turns from the story to the Prophet's situation, and it is a sobering line to place after such a complete and satisfying account.\n\nThe chapter has just delivered the finest of stories, filled with evidence of Allah's planning, forgiveness, patience, and reunion. And the verse that follows says most people will remain unconvinced.\n\nThe Arabic indicates the Prophet's eagerness, his intense desire for them to believe. This was not a man delivering a message reluctantly. Other verses describe him almost destroying himself with grief over their refusal, and Allah addressing that grief directly.\n\nThe verse does two things at once. It tells him the truth about what to expect, and it releases him from responsibility for the outcome. Both are acts of kindness.\n\nThere is a lesson here about the relationship between evidence and belief. If persuasion depended on the quality of the argument, this chapter should have converted everyone who heard it. It did not, and the Quran states plainly that it would not.\n\nThe reason lies in what the earlier chapters explained repeatedly. People whose refusal has hardened do not respond to proof, and belief involves the will as much as the mind. A person unwilling to accept the consequences of a truth will find a way past any amount of evidence.\n\nFor anyone who calls others to good today, whether a parent, a teacher, or a friend, this verse addresses a familiar disappointment. Presenting something clearly and beautifully, with genuine care for the person receiving it, does not guarantee acceptance.\n\nThat reality can produce bitterness, exhaustion, or the temptation to compromise the message to make it more appealing. The Quran offers a different response: keep delivering it honestly, expect that many will refuse, and understand that the refusal is not a verdict on the message or on you.\n\nThe following verse adds the detail that removes any excuse the refusers might have offered.",
  "104": "\"Even though you are not asking them for a reward for this ˹Quran˺\" removes the most obvious objection anyone could raise against the Prophet, and it does so at the very end of the chapter.\n\nA person hearing a religious message naturally asks what the messenger stands to gain. Payment, influence, and status are the usual answers, and any of them would weaken the message considerably. The Quran points out that none applies here.\n\nThis same declaration was made by Noah, Hud, Salih, Lut, and Shu'aib in the preceding chapter, each telling his people that his reward was only with Allah. The repetition establishes it as a mark of genuine prophethood.\n\nIn Mecca the claim was open to inspection. The Prophet had been offered wealth and leadership in exchange for stopping, and he refused. He endured boycott, insult, and the loss of his protection, and he collected nothing for any of it.\n\n\"It is only a reminder to the whole world.\" states what the Quran is and who it is for.\n\nThe word for reminder is significant. It suggests that the message is not entirely new information but a recalling of something people already have some sense of: that there is a Creator, that life has a purpose, that actions have consequences. Revelation awakens what heedlessness has buried.\n\n\"To the whole world\" defines the audience, and the Arabic covers all peoples and all times. This is a substantial claim for a message delivered in Arabic in a small trading town, to an audience of a few hundred, most of whom were hostile.\n\nPlacing it here, at the close of a chapter about a Hebrew prophet in Egypt, told to Arabs in Mecca, and read now across every continent, gives the claim a certain weight. The story of Joseph belongs to no single nation, and neither does the reminder it was sent to deliver.",
  "105": "\"How many signs in the heavens and the earth do they pass by with indifference!\" is an exclamation rather than a question, and it captures one of the strangest features of human life.\n\nThe signs are described as being everywhere, above and below, and the point of the verse is not that people cannot see them. It is that they walk past them. The Arabic indicates turning away from something while it is right there in front of you.\n\nFamiliarity is the mechanism. A person who has watched the sun rise ten thousand times stops noticing it. Rain becomes weather rather than a miracle of water lifted from the sea and dropped on dry land. A newborn child is a wonder for a few weeks and then becomes routine. Nothing about the signs has changed; the attention has faded.\n\nThis is why the Quran keeps returning to ordinary things, the alternation of night and day, the growth of plants from dead earth, the differences between people's languages and colours. It is not trying to inform anyone that these things exist. It is trying to make people look at what they have stopped seeing.\n\nPlacing this verse immediately after the story of Joseph is deliberate. That story was full of moments where Allah's arrangement was invisible to the people inside it: a caravan stopping for water, a forgotten promise, a cup in a bag. Only from the end can the pattern be seen.\n\nThe same is true of the world itself. Signs are not rare events that interrupt normal life. They are normal life, examined honestly.\n\nThere is a practical remedy implied in the complaint. Attention can be trained. A person who deliberately pauses over what he usually ignores, a plant, a hand, a night sky, is doing something the Quran repeatedly instructs. Surah Ali Imran describes people of understanding as those who remember Allah standing, sitting, and lying on their sides, and reflect on the creation of the heavens and the earth.",
  "106": "\"And most of them do not believe in Allah without associating others with Him ˹in worship˺\" makes a claim that surprises many readers when they first understand it.\n\nIt says that most believers in God do not believe purely. Their belief is mixed with something else.\n\nThis describes the situation in Mecca precisely. The Quraysh were not atheists. They acknowledged Allah as creator, provider, and controller of rain and life, and the Quran records them saying so. What they added was a layer of intermediaries: idols they said would bring them closer to Allah, deities they invoked for particular needs, and inherited practices that assigned some of His rights to others.\n\nThe Arabic word \"shirk\" means associating a partner with Allah, giving to something created what belongs only to the Creator. It does not require denying God. It requires dividing what should be undivided.\n\nUnderstanding this widens the verse considerably. Openly worshipping a statue is only the most visible form. Scholars have long discussed subtler forms, including performing worship for the sake of people's admiration, which the Prophet described as the hidden form of associating partners in a hadith recorded by Ahmad. Placing ultimate trust in wealth, in a person, or in one's own cleverness belongs to the same family of errors.\n\nThere is a useful test in this for anyone examining their own belief. It is not enough to ask whether a person believes in Allah, since most of humanity always has. The question is whether anything else has been allowed to share His place: as the source of provision, the object of real fear, the thing whose approval genuinely governs behaviour.\n\nPlacing this verse after the story of Joseph gives it context. Joseph in the prison had put the same question to two men who certainly believed in gods, asking whether many scattered lords were better than the One, the Supreme. That was the argument then, and this verse says it remains the argument now.",
  "107": "\"Do they feel secure that an overwhelming torment from Allah will not overtake them?\" asks about a particular kind of confidence, and it identifies a state of mind rather than a belief.\n\nThe people described are not necessarily denying that punishment exists. They feel safe from it. The Arabic indicates being secure, untroubled, free of any concern that something might come.\n\nThat sense of security is the target of the verse. It develops naturally when nothing has happened for a long time. Life continues, trade prospers, and the warnings people once heard begin to sound like background noise.\n\nThe Arabic word for the torment described here indicates something that covers and envelops, arriving over a whole community rather than picking off individuals.\n\n\"Or that the Hour will not take them by surprise when they least expect ˹it˺?\" adds the second possibility. Beyond any punishment within history lies the Hour itself, and its defining feature in the Quran is suddenness. It arrives while people are in the middle of ordinary business, as other verses describe, with no announcement.\n\nThe pairing of these two questions covers every timescale. Something may come to a community, and something will certainly come to everyone.\n\nThere is a state of mind the Quran consistently recommends instead, which is neither despair nor complacency. Islamic teaching describes the believer as living between hope and fear, expecting Allah's mercy while never assuming immunity. Both are necessary. Fear alone crushes a person; security alone makes him careless.\n\nThe Quran mentions this false security elsewhere in similar terms, noting that none feels safe from the plan of Allah except the people who are losers.\n\nFor the Meccan audience, the question had a particular edge. They lived beside the ruins of destroyed peoples and travelled past them regularly, and they still assumed that whatever happened to those nations had nothing to do with them.",
  "108": "\"\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"This is my way.\"\" is a declaration of position, and it comes at the point where the chapter turns from the story to the mission.\n\nThe Arabic word for way indicates a path or road, something with a direction and a destination. He is not describing an opinion but a course of life.\n\n\"I invite to Allah with insight\" defines the method, and both halves matter. The invitation is to Allah, not to a personality, a tribe, or a political programme. And it is offered with insight, using an Arabic word that indicates clear-sighted understanding, knowing what you are calling to and why.\n\nThat second element is a standard the Quran sets for anyone who speaks in the name of religion. Enthusiasm without knowledge causes damage, and history is full of well-meaning people who misrepresented what they were defending. The verse ties calling to Allah with actually understanding the matter.\n\n\"I and those who follow me\" extends the responsibility beyond the Prophet. Everyone who follows him shares this work, according to their ability and their knowledge. This is not a task reserved for scholars, though it does require that a person speak only about what he genuinely knows.\n\nThere is a balance in that. The verse does not say that only experts may speak, and it does not licence anyone to speak about anything. Each person conveys what he understands, with insight into that much.\n\n\"Glory be to Allah\" declares Him free of every fault and every partner, which is the content of the invitation in a single phrase.\n\n\"And I am not one of the polytheists.\" draws the line at the end, exactly as the preceding chapters did. The message is defined by what it affirms and by what it refuses.\n\nA believer today can read this verse as a job description. Know what you are talking about, invite people to Allah rather than to yourself, and keep your own belief clear of anything that competes with Him.",
  "109": "\"We only sent before you ˹O Prophet˺ men inspired by Us from among the people of each society\" answers an objection the Meccans raised repeatedly: that a messenger from God should not be an ordinary man from their own streets.\n\nThree points are packed into the sentence. Messengers were men, not angels. They received revelation rather than inventing their message. And they came from among the communities they addressed, so nobody could claim the messenger did not understand them.\n\nThe wording also places the Prophet in a line rather than presenting him as an innovation. Everything the Meccans found strange about him was true of every prophet before him.\n\n\"Have the deniers not travelled through the land to see what was the end of those ˹destroyed˺ before them?\" turns to evidence they could examine physically. The Quraysh were merchants whose caravans passed the ruins of earlier peoples on the routes north and south.\n\nThe invitation is to travel and look, which the Quran issues several times. This is not an appeal to accept a claim on authority. It is an appeal to observe what remains of communities that made the same choices.\n\n\"And surely the ˹eternal˺ Home of the Hereafter is far better for those mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" shifts from the ruins to the comparison that matters. Whatever those nations built, and some built impressively, none of it lasted. What is offered to those who are mindful of Allah does.\n\n\"Will you not then understand?\" closes with the same appeal to reason found throughout these chapters. The Arabic asks whether they will use their intellect.\n\nThat is a striking way to end an argument about faith. The Quran does not ask them to suspend their thinking. It asks them to apply it: look at history, notice the pattern, compare what lasts with what does not, and draw the conclusion themselves.",
  "110": "\"And when the messengers despaired and their people thought the messengers had been denied help, Our help came to them ˹at last˺\" describes the lowest point in a prophet's mission, and it is an unusually honest verse.\n\nThe messengers reached a stage of despair. Commentators have discussed this carefully, since prophets do not despair of Allah's mercy. The explanation generally given is that they despaired of their people responding, having exhausted every approach over many years, and that they had no expectation of when relief would arrive.\n\nThere is an alternative reading of the Arabic in which it is the people who assumed the messengers had been lied to, but the sense of the verse is the same either way: a point of maximum darkness, when nothing suggested that help was coming.\n\nThe chapter just concluded illustrates this precisely. Joseph in the well, Joseph in the prison after being forgotten for years, Jacob blind and mocked as senile. At no point in any of those situations was rescue visible.\n\n\"Our help came to them ˹at last˺\" is the turn, and its placement is the entire lesson. Help arrives at the point when human calculation has run out, not before.\n\nThis tells us something about how Allah's assistance works, and it is not what most people would design. Relief that comes early confirms a person's own planning. Relief that comes at the end confirms something else.\n\n\"We then saved whoever We willed\" describes the outcome for the believers. The wording preserves Allah's freedom in the matter, and the chapters preceding this one showed prophets and their followers being rescued while everyone else was removed.\n\n\"And Our punishment is never averted from the wicked people.\" completes the pair. Nothing turns it back once it comes.\n\nFor the Prophet's community in Mecca, living under boycott with no visible prospect of change, this verse was telling them exactly where they stood in the sequence: at the point before the help arrives.",
  "111": "\"In their stories there is truly a lesson for people of reason.\" closes the chapter by returning to the purpose stated near its beginning. The Arabic word for lesson indicates something a person crosses over from, moving from the surface of an event to the meaning behind it.\n\nThe word \"reason\" identifies who benefits. The Quran repeatedly attaches its evidence to those willing to think, and this verse ends a chapter that has given them a great deal to consider.\n\n\"This message cannot be a fabrication\" states the claim directly. A story of this length, detail, and coherence, delivered by an unlettered man in Mecca, including private conversations from centuries earlier in another land, does not have a human explanation.\n\n\"Rather ˹it is˺ a confirmation of previous revelation\" gives the first of four descriptions. The Quran agrees with the essential content of what was revealed before, and the story of Joseph was known in outline to the Jewish communities of Arabia, though this version differs from theirs in important ways.\n\n\"A detailed explanation of all things\" is the second. The Arabic indicates laying matters out clearly, part by part. This does not mean the Quran contains every fact about every subject; it means it explains fully what people need for guidance.\n\n\"A guide\" is the third, pointing to the road. Explanation without direction leaves a person informed and still lost.\n\n\"And a mercy for people of faith\" is the fourth and final description, and it ends the chapter where the story itself ended: with mercy.\n\nLooking back over the chapter, that word covers all of it. A boy rescued from a well, a man protected in a locked room, a prisoner remembered at exactly the right moment, a family reunited after decades, and brothers forgiven by the person they had wronged most.\n\nThe Quran calls itself a mercy, and it has just spent a hundred and eleven verses demonstrating what that looks like in a single human life."
};
